window.ROSETTA_SEARCH = [{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/3DPrintableFiles.html","m":"User Manual","t":"3D Printable Parts","h":[["Cable-Marking-Plates","Cable Marking Plates"],["Printing-Notes","Printing Notes"],["Compatibility-Notes","Compatibility Notes"]],"x":"Cable Marking Plates Cable Marking Plates These are useful for attaching to the cables coming from the stepper motors at the end where they are plugged into the Rosetta box. This makes identifying which plug is which is easier. REB_Cable_Marking_Plates.3mf These print each plate as 2” x ⅝”, and can be attached to the cable using small cable ties. Printing Notes These should be printed with the text down. That gives the best, flattest surface for the plate’s surface which is seen. Compatibility Notes They are provided as a 3mf file which is formatted for printing on a Bambu Lab printer with multiple filament support. This enables the text to be embedded into the front as a different color. These were tested on a Bambu Labs P1S with an AMS."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/AI.html","m":"User Manual","t":"AI - Artificial Intelligence","h":[["What-Claude-can-do-for-you","What Claude can do for you"],["Write-G-code","Write G-code"],["Make-G-code-run-faster","Make G-code run faster"],["Explain-and-check-G-code","Explain and check G-code"],["Troubleshoot-errors-and-tune-the-machine","Troubleshoot errors and tune the machine"],["Learn","Learn"],["Two-ways-to-use-Claude","Two ways to use Claude"],["Using-Claude-Code-on-the-Raspberry-Pi","Using Claude Code on the Raspberry Pi"],["Things-to-try","Things to try"],["Using-Claude-safely","Using Claude safely"]],"x":"Anthropic’s Claude Code is installed on the Raspberry Pi in your Rosetta. Claude is an AI assistant: you describe what you want in plain English, and it helps you get there - writing and checking G-code, explaining what a program does, and working out what went wrong when the machine stops with an error. To use Claude you need your own Claude account with a Claude Pro (or higher) subscription from claude.com . The subscription covers both Claude on the web and Claude Code on the Raspberry Pi; a license for Claude is not included with Rosetta. What Claude can do for you Write G-code Describe the pattern you want and Claude drafts the program. For example: Write G-code to cut a 24-lobe rosette pattern on the face of a 3 inch blank, 0.020 inch deep, with the X axis moving in and out as C turns. It can lay the program out with named parameters (#<_Depth>, #<_Lobes>, ...) at the top, so you can change the design without rewriting the moves, and it adds comments explaining each section. Make G-code run faster Claude can review a program and explain where the time goes. A real example: one rosette program took about 4 hours to run. Reviewing it with Claude showed that it was made of about 80,000 very short moves of the C axis, so its speed was limited by C's acceleration, not its top speed - and that each pass ended with a full 360° rapid back to the start. Raising C's acceleration and speed in the Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning tab brought the estimate down to about 45 minutes , and Claude also suggested a change to the program itself to remove the rewinds. Other things Claude looks for: needless rapid moves, many tiny moves where fewer, longer ones would do, feed rates set lower than they need to be, and repeated sections that could be a subroutine. Explain and check G-code Paste in (or point Claude Code at) a program you were given and ask what it does, line by line. Claude can also check a program before you run it - for moves that go further than your machine can travel, a missing feed rate, units (G20/G21) that don't match your machine, or an axis letter your machine isn't set up to use. Troubleshoot errors and tune the machine When LinuxCNC stops with a message such as \"Joint 5 following error\" , tell Claude what you were doing and what the message said. It can explain the likely causes in plain terms - for example, that a following error right at the start of a program often just means that axis wasn't enabled - and walk you through the fix. It can also help you work out settings such as an axis's Scale from your motor, drive and leadscrew or gearing, and suggest sensible Max Speed and Max Acceleration values to try in the Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning tab. Learn Ask about anything: what G93 inverse-time mode is for, how the Indexing tab's Div setting works, or how a rose engine pumping pattern is built up. Claude answers at whatever level you ask for. Two ways to use Claude Claude on the web Claude Code on the Raspberry Pi Where In a web browser at claude.ai , on the Raspberry Pi which is inside the Rosetta box, or on any computer, tablet, or phone. In a Terminal Emulator window on Rosetta’s Raspberry Pi. What it can see Only what you paste in or attach (a G-code file, a screenshot, an error message). The files on the Raspberry Pi - your G-code, Rosetta’s own files and its manual notes - so it already knows how your machine is set up. What it can do Answer questions and write or rewrite text and G-code for you to copy back. Everything the web version can, plus read and save files and run commands on the Raspberry Pi - always asking you first. Best for Questions and planning, especially away from the shop. Working on your actual programs and your actual machine. Both use the same Claude account and subscription. Using Claude Code on the Raspberry Pi Open a Terminal Emulator. Move to Rosetta's folder - or to its gcode folder if you are working on programs: cd ~/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode Starting Claude here matters: in this folder Claude reads Rosetta’s own notes, so it knows how the machine works. Start Claude by entering on the command line: claude The first time, Claude asks you to log in: choose your Claude account and follow the steps in the browser window it opens. Type what you want in plain English and press Enter. When Claude wants to save a file or run a command, it shows you exactly what it will do and waits for you to say yes or no. To exit Claude, enter within Claude: /exit For help with Claude Code itself, enter /help . Things to try \"Explain what Pumping5Sides.ngc does, section by section.\" \"This program takes 4 hours. Why, and how could it run faster without changing the pattern?\" \"Write a program that indexes the spindle 24 times, cutting a 0.010 inch flute on each division.\" \"LinuxCNC stopped with 'Joint 2 following error' while jogging B. What should I check?\" \"My X axis moves 0.098 inch when I ask for 0.100. What should its Scale be?\" Using Claude safely Check every new or changed program before cutting. Look at it in the preview, then run it with the cutter clear of the work (an \"air cut\"). Claude is very good, but it can make mistakes, and it can't see your machine. Don't change the machine while it is running a job. Ask Claude to look at files and logs freely, but only let it change settings or files when the machine is stopped. Use the Rosetta Settings program for settings - not Claude editing the settings file (RosettaSet_v1.ini) by hand. Keep your own work in the gcode folder. Changes Claude makes to Rosetta’s own program files can be set aside by the next update . Your G-code, your tool table and your HAL additions (Rosetta_Custom/Rosetta_Custom.hal) are kept. Don't share passwords or private information with Claude."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/Axes.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Axes","h":[["Base-Box","Base Box"],["Expansion-Box","Expansion Box"],["Setting-the-axis-assignments","Setting the axis assignments"],["Device-specific-axis-assignments","Device-specific axis assignments"],["Device-specific-axis-scenario-Cross-slide","Device-specific axis scenario - Cross slide"],["Device-specific-axis-scenario-Curvilinear-slide","Device-specific axis scenario - Curvilinear slide"],["Why-use-device-specific-files","Why use device-specific files?"]],"x":"Lathe Axes , shown on a Lindow Rose Engine Lathe Axes , shown on an MDF Rose Engine 2.0 The Rosetta system can support up to 10 axes (6 on the Base Box and 4 more on the optional Expansion Box). The stepper motor plugs on the system are known as channels. Each channel can be set to one of the spindles or axes noted below. These axes are also depicted in the pictures to the right to show how they orient for use on lathes. Lathe Axes Axis Mvmt Type Potential Use(s) Comments Sp0 Spindle Spindle Sp1 Spindle Rosette Phaser/Multiplier X Linear Cross Slide By convention, this is in and out Y n/a n/a - axis is not configured Z Linear Cross Slide Curvilinear Slide By convention, this is left and right A Rotary By convention, A is rotation around X B Rotary Spherical Slide By convention, B is rotation around Y C Rotary Spindle By convention, C is rotation around Z. C is also used when synchronized motion of another axis and the spindle is needed (the spindle’s motor is plugged into this axis jack). U Linear Retractor Dynamic Rubber U is parallel to X V Linear Dynamic Amplitude Adjuster V is parallel to Y By convention, this is up and down. W Linear Cross Slide Curvilinear Slide W is parallel to Z Axis Assignments on Base Box Axis Assignments with Expansion Box Base Box On the Base Box, up to 6 of these spindles and axes can be selected for use at any time - one per channel (plug). Sp1 can only be used when Sp0 is also selected for use. The axes selected for use are shown at the bottom of the LinuxCNC screen on the right. As depicted here, the selected axes are: U, V, W, C, X, & Z. Expansion Box With the addition of the Expansion Box, all 10 of these spindles and axes can be selected for use at any time - one per channel (plug). The Rosetta user can move the axes around to be assigned to whichever channel they wish. Setting the axis assignments To update the assignment of axes to channels (= plugs on the front of the box), you need to use the Rosetta Settings program (the one with a gear as the icon). Rosetta Settings allows you to select Which axes you wish to use, and Which channel each axis is assigned to use. The process to do this is Exit Rosetta Start Rosetta Settings Make whatever changes you wish, Save the settings, then Exit Rosetta Settings Restart Rosetta . Device-specific axis assignments Device-specific settings are a very useful feature. You may find that your curvilinear slide's stepper motor runs differently than your cross slide. And as they both will probably be setup using the Z axis, you don't have figure it out each time. For example: Cross Slide Curvilinear Slide Leadscrew Has a 0.100” pitch Has 1 start Gearing ratio is 120:20 Leadscrew Moves with a 2mm pitch Has 4 starts (thusly it moves 8 mm per revolution) Gearing ratio is 1:1 For my cross slide, the calculated value was different than the actual value. Additionally, the two axes were not the same. The values were calibrated using a dial indicator to be: X Axis Scale = 387,650 pulses/inch Backlash = 0.027” Z Axis Scale = 387,100 pulses/inch Backlash = 0.027” Scale = 2,015.748 pulses/inch Backlash = 0.005” Device-specific axis scenario - Cross slide Using the Rosetta Settings program, enter the values you want for the Cross Slide. For this scenario that includes X axis Z axis Once everything is good, use the button, Save Subset of Settings As... to save the settings for only these axes to its own file. I named mine, \"CrossSlide.RosettaSet_v1.ini\". You can name it anything, but it cannot be named RosettaSet_v1.ini . And it should have an extension of .ini. Then, whenever you want to use your cross slide, you can load the settings for it. The re-loaded file will only change the values for those axes (in this case, X & Z) and all the others will stay the same. Device-specific axis scenario - Curvilinear slide Using the Rosetta Settings program, you can enter the values you want for the Curvilinear Slide. For this scenario that includes Z axis Once everything is good, use the button, Save Subset of Settings As... to save the settings for only the Z axis to its own file. I named mine, \"CurvilinearSlide.RosettaSet_v1.ini\". As noted above, you can name it anything, but it cannot be named RosettaSet_v1.ini . And it should have an extension of .ini. Then, whenever you want to use your curvilinear slide, you can re-load those settings for it. The re-loaded file will only change the values for that axis (in this case, Z) and all the others will stay the same. Why use device-specific files? There are a number of reasons for doing this: It makes it easy to switch devices and easily load the settings needed for that device It allows you to save the values for your devices in case you accidentally over-write them It allows a device maker to supply the data with the device"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/AxisAndStepperMotorTuning.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning","h":[["Basic-Settings","Basic Settings"],["Scale_","Scale"],["Microsteps-and-Max-Speed","Microsteps and Max Speed"],["Max-Speed","Max Speed"],["Max-Acceleration","Max Acceleration"],["Backlash_","Backlash"],["The-warnings-line","The warnings line"],["Advanced-Settings","Advanced Settings"],["Linear-and-Rotary-Angular-axes","Linear and Rotary/Angular axes"],["Spindles","Spindles"],["Restore-Defaults","Restore Defaults"],["Running-the-Rosetta-Settings-Program","Running the Rosetta Settings Program"],["Option-1-Use-the-icon-on-the-desktop","Option 1: Use the icon on the desktop"],["Option-2-Use-the-Applications-menu","Option 2: Use the Applications menu"]],"x":"Key Note: The configuration program cannot be run when the Rosetta program is running. It is designed so that you configure the way Rosetta works, then run the system. Basic Settings Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning This page has three groups: Linear axes (X, Z, U, V, W), Rotary/Angular axes (A, B, C), and Spindles (Sp0, Sp1). Each axis has its own column, and each setting has its own row. The column to the right of each row explains that row. There are fields for every axis, including the ones not currently being used. Only the values for the axes being used make any difference. Each group shows these rows: Row Linear and Rotary/Angular axes Spindles Scale Yes Yes Max Speed / Max Vel Max Speed Max Vel Max Acceleration / Max Accel Max Acceleration Max Accel Backlash Yes Yes Scale This is the number of pulses Rosetta sends to the stepper drive to move the axis one unit: one inch (or mm) for a linear axis, one degree for a rotary/angular axis, and one revolution for a spindle. For example, in the screen to the right, 57,600 pulses are needed for the Sp0 spindle to turn 360°. Scale = microsteps per motor turn (set by the switches on the stepper drive) × motor turns per unit (from the gearing and the leadscrew) Rosetta's stepper drives are normally set to 6,400 microsteps per motor turn. To work out the Scale for each axis and check it by measuring, see Scale Settings for a Device . If the axis moves the wrong way, change the sign of the Scale (+ to -, or - to +). Microsteps and Max Speed Rosetta can send at most 200,000 pulses a second to each stepper drive (the pulse timing the drives need). So the bigger the Scale, the slower the axis's fastest speed: Fastest speed = 200,000 ÷ Scale Example Scale Fastest speed X: 6,400 microsteps, 6 : 1 pulleys, 10 turns per inch 384,000 per inch 0.52 inch / second The same X with the drive set to 3,200 microsteps 192,000 per inch 1.04 inch / second Sp0: 6,400 microsteps, 9 : 1 pulleys 57,600 per revolution 3.47 revolutions / second (208 rpm) If an axis's Max Speed is more than this, the warnings line (see below) says Max Speed is more than the step timing allows at this Scale . Either lower Max Speed, or set the drive to fewer microsteps (half the microsteps = half the Scale = twice the fastest speed) and change the Scale to match. Do not change the microstep switches on a stepper drive without changing that axis's Scale to match. If you do, every move on that axis is the wrong size - half, or double, what you asked for. Note: Fewer microsteps let an axis go faster, but it moves less smoothly at slow speeds. For most rose engine work - slow, smooth movement - leave the drives at 6,400. The motor itself can also run out of torque at high speed, before this limit is reached. To understand why, and how to choose the microstep setting, see Stepper Motor: Microstepping and Speed in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge. Max Speed This is the fastest the axis may move - in a program, an MDI command, or a jog. It is measured in distance per second: inches (or mm) per second for a linear axis, and degrees per second for a rotary/angular axis. For example, 30 degrees / second = 5 revolutions per minute. The Max Speed of a linear axis cannot be set above 1 inch / second (25.4 mm / second). For the spindles, this row is called Max Vel , and it is measured in revolutions per second. For example, 0.167 revolutions / second = 10 revolutions per minute. Note: When you change Max Speed, the advanced settings worked out from it ( Stepgen Max Vel and PID Max Output - see below) change with it, so you do not need to open the advanced settings. Max Acceleration This is how quickly the axis may speed up and slow down. It is measured in distance per second 2 : inches (or mm) per second 2 for a linear axis, and degrees per second 2 for a rotary/angular axis. For the spindles, this row is called Max Accel , and it is measured in revolutions per second 2 . A program made up of many short moves (such as a rosette pattern on C) finishes sooner with a higher Max Acceleration, because each short move spends most of its time speeding up and slowing down. Note: When you change Max Acceleration, Stepgen Max Accel (see below) changes with it. Backlash Backlash is the slack in an axis's drive - play in the leadscrew, nut, gears or belt - which is taken up each time the axis changes direction, before the axis really moves. Rosetta makes up for it: each time the axis reverses, it first moves the motor this much further. Enter the axis's lost motion, measured with a dial indicator (see Set the Backlash First for how). 0 turns it off. A little less than the measured value is better than too much, which makes the axis jump each time it reverses. Note: Backlash is saved as soon as you change it. For more on backlash, and what compensation can't fix, see Backlash in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge. The warnings line Just under the Backlash row, each group has a line which is usually empty. If a value does not fit with another, a warning appears there in orange, naming the axis - for example: C: Stepgen Max Accel is below 1.5 x Max Acceleration The warnings are: Max Speed is more than the step timing allows at this Scale - the stepper drive cannot be sent pulses fast enough for that speed. Lower Max Speed (or, for a spindle, Max Vel). Stepgen Max Vel is below Max Speed , Stepgen Max Accel is below 1.5 x Max Acceleration , or PID Max Output is below Max Speed - an advanced setting is too low. The easiest fix is the axis's Restore Defaults button (see below). Indexing Max Output is above Max Vel (spindles only) - lower Indexing Max Output, or click the spindle's Restore Defaults button. Note: A warning does not stop you saving. It tells you that LinuxCNC may stop with an error, or that the axis may not reach its Max Speed. Advanced Settings Advanced Settings Each group has an Advanced settings twistie below the warnings line. It starts closed. Click it to show the advanced settings, and click it again to hide them. An example is shown to the right for the Linear axes. Most people never need to change these. Each is explained in the column to the right of its row. Linear and Rotary/Angular axes Row What it is Normally Stepgen Max Vel The step generator's own speed limit. Keep it at or above Max Speed. 1.25 x Max Speed Stepgen Max Accel The step generator's own acceleration limit. Keep it at least 1.5 x Max Acceleration. 2 x Max Acceleration PID Max Output The fastest the position loop may command. Keep it at or above Max Speed. 1.1 x Max Speed P, I, D, FF0, FF1, FF2 The position loop's PID gains. Restore Defaults A button for each axis - see below. Spindles The spindles' advanced settings are for indexing (turning the spindle to a set angle): Row What it is Indexing Max Output The fastest the spindle turns while indexing. Keep it at or below Max Vel. P, I, D The indexing loop's PID gains. Restore Defaults A button for each spindle - see below. Restore Defaults Each axis and each spindle has a Restore Defaults button at the bottom of its column. It puts that axis's advanced settings back to their normal values: Stepgen Max Vel, Stepgen Max Accel and PID Max Output are worked out again from the axis's own Max Speed and Max Acceleration, and the PID gains go back to Rosetta's starting values. (For a spindle: Indexing Max Output and the indexing PID gains.) Restore Defaults does not change Scale , Max Speed , Max Acceleration or Backlash . Do not forget to click Save All Settings after Restore Defaults. Like the other values on this page, the restored values are only kept when you click Save All Settings . Notes : More information is available at: LinuxCNC PID Component Reference PID Controller (Wikipedia) Every value on this page except Backlash is saved when you click Save All Settings (on the Axis Selection tab, or in the File menu). If you close the Settings program with changes that haven't been saved, it asks whether to save them first. Backlash is saved as soon as you change it. See Saving and Sharing Settings . Changes take effect the next time LinuxCNC starts. This page is bigger than the screen. Scroll with the mouse wheel, or use the bar on the right side of the page. Running the Rosetta Settings Program To access the Rosetta Settings program, there are 2 options: Option 1: Use the icon on the desktop Double-click on the Rosetta Settings icon on the desktop (the big gear) Option 2: Use the Applications menu Click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen Click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta Click on Rosetta Settings (the one with a gear) Applications Rosetta > Rosetta Rosetta Settings Backup Rosetta Update Rosetta Geometric Chuck Designer Rosette Designer"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/AxisSelection.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Axis Selection","h":[["Spindles","Spindles"],["Base-Box-and-Expansion-Box","Base Box and Expansion Box"],["Impact-of-Any-Changes","Impact of Any Changes"],["Running-the-Rosetta-Settings-Program","Running the Rosetta Settings Program"],["Option-1-Use-the-icon-on-the-desktop","Option 1: Use the icon on the desktop"],["Option-2-Use-the-Applications-menu","Option 2: Use the Applications menu"]],"x":"Key Note: The configuration program cannot be run when the Rosetta program is running. It is designed so that you configure the way Rosetta works, then run the system. Lathe Axes On this screen, you select which axes you want to use for the configurable ones. You must select from the standardized ① options available in LinuxCNC: Linear Axes X n/a (there is no Y axis option in LinuxCNC when configured as a lathe) Z U (parallel to the X axis) V (parallel to the Y axis) W (parallel to the Z axis) Rotary Axes A (rotation about the X axis) B (rotation about the Y axis) C (rotation about the Z axis) Spindles The spindles are specialized rotary axes. Sp0 is used for the primary spindle Sp1 is used for the rosette phaser / multiplier. Whilst these are the traditional interpretations of those axes, there is nothing particularly critical about them except that some are linear, and others rotary. So, if you need to implement a device which has 3 axes rotating around the spindle axis, you can use A, B, & C to do that -- you'll just have to keep everything straight. Additionally, you can select which channel (i.e., which plug) on the boxes is used for each axis. The only limitation is that Sp1 cannot be selected when Sp0 is not also selected . Axis Selection Screen Rosetta handles up to 10 channels - 6 on the base box and 4 more on the optional expansion box (see Base Box and Expansion Box below) - and any of the following can be assigned to them. Spindles The two spindles, Sp0 and Sp1, are used when you use the options on the main panel: Run Operation (to run the spindles in forward or reverse), or Indexing the spindles If those motors are needed for synchronized movement with other axes, This is done via a gCode program, and The spindles will need to use one of the rotary/angular axes (A, B, or C) for their motion. Base Box and Expansion Box Item Base Box Expansion Box Channels (plugs) 00 - 05 06 - 09 Limit switch jacks 1 - 3 4 - 6 E-stop button Yes n/a Signal tower Yes n/a Each plug's number matches its channel number. On the Axis Selection screen, the base box's channels are on the left and the expansion box's on the right, each laid out the way the plugs are on the box (Top and Bottom rows). The E-stop button and signal tower plug into the base box only. Connected - tick the Connected box next to \"Expansion Box\" only if the expansion box is plugged in. With it ticked, LinuxCNC looks for the expansion box when it starts. If it can't find it, LinuxCNC will fail to start. If that happens, untick Connected and start Rosetta again. Turn on both boxes before starting Rosetta. Ticking or unticking Connected takes effect the next time Rosetta starts. Without the expansion box its channels (and limit jacks 4 to 6) are greyed out, and the axes assigned to them can't be used. They keep their assignments, ready for when an expansion box is connected. To use one of those axes, choose it on a base box channel - the two swap, so the axis that was on that base box channel moves to the expansion box channel. For example, if Sp0 is on channel 06 and W is on channel 04, choosing Sp0 for channel 04 puts W on channel 06. Impact of Any Changes Axis Assignments on Base Box Axis Assignments with Expansion Box Once the changes are saved and Rosetta is restarted, changes made to this screen: Set the way LinuxCNC interacts with the plugs on the system (via the channel assignments) Update the axes displayed on the right side panel (an example is to the right) Set the options available for the axes on the right side panel (linear axes have different options than angular/rotary axes). Update the Axis Assignments table displayed at the bottom of the right side panel, which shows the axis on each channel. The expansion box's channels are only shown when it is connected. Any axes not assigned to a channel in use are grayed out and not usable by the operator. Running the Rosetta Settings Program To access the Rosetta Settings program, there are 2 options: Option 1: Use the icon on the desktop Double-click on the Rosetta Settings icon on the desktop (the big gear) Option 2: Use the Applications menu Click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen Click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta Click on Rosetta Settings (the one with a gear) Applications Rosetta > Rosetta Rosetta Settings Backup Rosetta Update Rosetta Geometric Chuck Designer Rosette Designer"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/BackingUpKeyUserFilesToAUSBMemoryStick.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Backing Up Key User Files to a USB Flash Drive","h":[["What-Gets-Copied","What Gets Copied"],["Settings","Settings"],["Gcode-Programs","Gcode Programs"],["HAL-Additions","HAL Additions"],["Your-Subroutines-and-User-M-codes","Your Subroutines and User M-codes"],["Backing-up-the-Rosetta-Settings-files","Backing up the Rosetta Settings files"],["Option-1-Use-the-Applications-menu","Option 1: Use the Applications menu"],["Option-2-Use-the-File-menu-in-Rosetta-Settings","Option 2: Use the File menu in Rosetta Settings"],["After-the-backup-finishes-the-USB-flash-drive-is-ejected","After the backup finishes: the USB flash drive is ejected"]],"x":"The Back Up to USB program copies your own Rosetta files onto a USB flash drive plugged into the Raspberry Pi. Each backup goes into its own dated folder, so earlier backups on the flash drive are never overwritten. To copy a backup back onto the Raspberry Pi - for example after replacing its memory card - see Restoring Key User Files from a USB Flash Drive . What Gets Copied These are the files which get backed up to a USB flash drive when this program gets run. Note: hidden files (names starting with a period) are not copied. Settings Your settings, and any settings files you have exported get backed up. Files Folder on the USB Flash Drive /home/reuben/Documents/*.ini Documents Key Note: Settings files (*.ini) saved in other directories are not backed up by this program. Gcode Programs Your gcode programs in the default directory. Files Folder on the USB Flash Drive /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode/*.* gcode Key Note: Gcode programs in sub-directories of this folder are backed up. However, gcode programs in other directories are not backed up by this program. HAL Additions Your own HAL additions and your tool table. Files Folder on the USB Flash Drive /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Custom/Rosetta_Custom.hal /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Custom/Rosetta_Tool.tbl Rosetta_Custom Key Note: If these files are saved in other directories, they are not backed up by this program. Your Subroutines and User M-codes Your own subroutines and user M-codes in LinuxCNC's nc_files directory. Files Folder on the USB Flash Drive /home/reuben/linuxcnc/nc_files/*.* nc_files Key Note: If these files are saved in other directories, they are not backed up by this program. Backing up the Rosetta Settings files To back up these files, there are 2 options. Option 1: Use the Applications menu Click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen Click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta Click on Backup Rosetta Applications Rosetta > Rosetta Rosetta Settings Backup Rosetta Update Rosetta Geometric Chuck Designer Rosette Designer Option 2: Use the File menu in Rosetta Settings Open the Rosetta Settings program. Click on File at the top left corner of the Rosetta Settings window. Click on Back Up to USB... The same Back Up to USB window opens. When you close it, you are back in Rosetta Settings. Note: Rosetta Settings cannot be run while LinuxCNC is running, so this option is only there when LinuxCNC is closed. After the backup finishes: the USB flash drive is ejected When the backup finishes, Rosetta ejects the USB flash drive. Ejecting means the Raspberry Pi has finished with the flash drive and has disconnected from it, so that it is safe to pull out. The flash drive is still plugged in, but the Raspberry Pi can no longer use it: it no longer shows in the File Manager, or in the Back Up to USB or Restore from USB windows. Do not pull the USB flash drive out while the backup is running. Wait until Rosetta says It is now safe to remove the USB stick. To use the USB flash drive again - for example, to make another backup, to restore a backup, or to copy a gcode program from it - you have to take it out and put it back in: Pull the USB flash drive out of the Raspberry Pi. Wait about 5 seconds. Plug the USB flash drive back into the Raspberry Pi. Wait a few seconds for the Raspberry Pi to find it. A File Manager window may open showing what is on the flash drive; you can close that window. The USB flash drive can now be used again. Note: If Rosetta says The stick could not be ejected automatically , eject it yourself before pulling it out: open the File Manager, and in the list on the left side of its window, click the eject symbol (⏏) next to the USB flash drive's name. When the flash drive's name disappears from the list, it is safe to pull it out."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/E-Stop.html","m":"User Manual","t":"E-Stop (F1)","h":[["Lost-Contact-with-a-Box","Lost Contact with a Box"],["E-Stop-Button","E-Stop Button"],["Using-the-E-Stop-Button","Using the E-Stop Button"]],"x":"E-STOP is always engaged when the system is started. You must disengage the E-STOP before any other operations can be performed. This can be done by either clicking on the indicated icon, or by using the F1 key. Either will toggle this state. Key Note : If something is going awry, use the F1 button to put the machine into an E-STOP mode. That will stop all movement. And, when the E-STOP status is reset, the settings you were using will be waiting for you to resume or restart the operation. Lost Contact with a Box Rosetta also goes into E-STOP by itself if the Raspberry Pi loses contact with the Base Box or the Expansion Box while it is running - for example, a network cable comes loose, or a box is switched off. All the axes stop, a message such as Expansion box lost contact - E-stop says which box it was, and (if used) the signal tower's red light comes on and the fault siren sounds. Check that box's network cable, the network switch in the Base Box, and the box's power. Once the box is back in contact, press F1 to clear the E-STOP. E-Stop Button If an E-stop button is connected, pressing it also puts the system into E-STOP, and the E-STOP cannot be released (F1) until the button is released. Using the E-Stop Button Machine State E-Stop Button Status Action Taken What Happens Emergency Stop Engaged Press the F1 button Nothing happens. These cannot clear having the E-Stop button engaged. Click the Emergency Stop icon Disengage E-Stop Button Nothing happens. The E-Stop button will not toggle the Emergency Stop condition to off. Disengaged Press the F1 button The Emergency Stop status is cleared. Click the Emergency Stop icon Machine is running Disengaged Engage the E-Stop Button The machine drops into Emergency Stop Press the F1 button Click the Emergency Stop icon"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/E-StopCabling.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Cabling an E-Stop Button to Rosetta","h":[["Connector","Connector"],["Cable-Sizing","Cable Sizing"],["Wiring-the-Switch","Wiring the Switch"],["E-Stop-Button-Notes","E-Stop Button Notes"]],"x":"GX-16/2 Plug Connector A GX-16/2 female plug is used to connect an E-stop button to the Rosetta system. This plug is sometimes called an aviation connector . Cable Sizing The cable between the Rosetta system and your E-stop button should: Have 2 conductors within the cable Be stranded - this is more flexible than solid wire Be 22 AWG (gauge; 24 AWG is the minimum) - this is easily soldered to the GX-16 connector Be shielded, with the shield connected to ground at the Rosetta box end only - this will help isolate the signal from noise which could be encountered in adjacent cables (e.g., for stepper motors) Wiring the Switch GX-16 Pin Wire Color Purpose 1 Red Signal (to the Rosetta input) - do not connect to any power source 2 Black GND (ground) E-Stop Button Notes E-stop switches are wired as normally closed , and the circuit becomes open when the switch is triggered. The E-stop button simply connects the two pins together when it is not pressed. The Rosetta box supplies the signal itself, so never connect either wire to a power supply. The E-stop button plugs into the Base Box. The use of such a switch must be configured on the Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab of the Rosetta Settings program. See E-Stop for how the button works together with the F1 key."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/ENAble.html","m":"User Manual","t":"ENAble","h":[],"x":"When we note that an axis is enabled, it really means that the stepper motor on that axis is enabled. With stepper motors, the motor for an axis can be enabled or not. Axis Status Motor Shaft Can be Rotated by Hand Motor Can Be Operated by LinuxCNC Comments ENA No Yes Axis is Enabled An axis must be enabled for Rosetta to drive the motor, making the motor rotate its shaft. When performing indexing operations, it is recommended to leave the axis enabled between indexing movements. This helps ensure there is no movement before the next indexing operation. There is a configuration setting which is related to this. Axis Not Enabled can be set to warn the user if the a needed axis is not enabled, or to automatically enable the needed axes. ENA Yes No Axis is not Enabled When an axis is not enabled, the motor’s shaft can be turned by hand. This is a useful option if there is a hand wheel used to position the cutter manually. An example of this is for cross slides and curvilinear slides. This setting allows the axis to be automatically enabled whenever needed. If an axis is automatically enabled for use, it is not un-enabled when the operation completes. Un-enabling the axis must be done manually. ENA Yes No Axis is not configured for use Within the Settings program, the Axis Selection tab can be used to make an axis availlable for use."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/EnteringData.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Entering Data","h":[],"x":"Whenever you want to change a value in the Rosetta screens, keep these thoughts in mind: You must have your mouse over the data field to type in a new value You must depress the Enter key after entering any data for the system to accept that you have entered a new value. These are all a function of the programming system we used for the display panels (and this is the most advanced programming system available)."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/GeometricChuckDesigner.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Geometric Chuck Designer","h":[["Opening-the-Geometric-Chuck-Designer","Opening the Geometric Chuck Designer"],["Designing-a-Pattern","Designing a Pattern"],["Chuck-Settings","Chuck Settings"],["Bazley-s-Figures","Bazley's Figures"],["Stages_","Stages"],["Movement_","Movement"],["Depth_","Depth"],["Advance-and-Retract-Cutter","Advance and Retract Cutter"],["Notes-Under-the-Drawings","Notes Under the Drawings"],["Setting-the-Start-Radius-Check-Circle","Setting the Start Radius: Check Circle"],["Saving-and-Running-the-Program","Saving and Running the Program"],["Changing-a-Saved-Pattern","Changing a Saved Pattern"]],"x":"A geometric chuck stacks circular motions - wheels on wheels - to cut looping, star-like and lobed patterns on the face of the work (see the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge's Geometric Chuck page). The Geometric Chuck Designer makes a software geometric chuck: you set it up as you would a two-part geometric chuck - or choose one of T. S. Bazley's 3,400 figures by its number - see the pattern it makes, and it saves a G-code program in which C turns the work while X moves the cutter in and out to trace that pattern on the face. Use it to try patterns before cutting them, or to cut patterns without a geometric chuck at all. Acknowledgement: The chuck settings, and the settings for every one of Bazley's figures, come from Roy Lindley , who has studied the geometric chuck for more than 15 years and generously shared his work with the Rosetta project. Thank you, Roy. Opening the Geometric Chuck Designer Click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen. Click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta. Click on Geometric Chuck Designer . The Geometric Chuck Designer only writes G-code files, so it can be open while Rosetta is running. Only one Geometric Chuck Designer window can be open. If one is already open, for example behind Rosetta, opening it again brings that window to the front, with a message saying it was already open. Designing a Pattern The Geometric Chuck Designer fills the screen, like the Rosette Designer. The drawings in the middle change as you change the settings on the left, and the notes on the right explain each section, with a button that opens this page at that section. The drawings: The pattern shows the cut on the face of the work, seen from the end. The red dot is where the cut starts. X and C shows how X (the cutter's radius) and C (the angle) move along the pattern. Chuck Settings A two-part geometric chuck adds three circles together, each turning at its own speed. The pattern is where they add up. Set by chooses how you enter them: Chuck settings (L, V) - as you would set a geometric chuck (below). This is the usual way. Stages (r, k) - the circles one by one, for patterns a two-part chuck can't make (see Stages , below). The two give the same pattern where both can make it, and switching between them keeps the pattern. L1, L2, L3 The radii of the three circles, in inches (or mm). Leave L3 at 0 for a one-stage pattern. V1, V2 The chuck's two ratios - how fast the second and third circles turn: the second turns 1 - V1 times for each turn of the first, and the third (1 - V1) + V1 × V2 times. A whole number (6), a negative number (-4) or a fraction (7/3). V2 only matters when L3 isn't 0. If you type something that isn't a number, the box turns red and the note under the drawings says so; the last good value is used until you correct it. G3 Where the third circle starts, in degrees. Changing it changes the pattern: a figure and its companion differ only by G3 = 0 or 180. Rotation Turns the whole figure, in degrees. (Indexing C on the Rosetta panel before running the program does the same.) Presets Fills in a classic pattern to start from: an ellipse, 5 lobes, a 5-point star, loops through the centre, a pattern that closes after 3 turns, a three-circle pattern, and 3 petals through the centre. Choosing V1. With two circles (L3 = 0), the pattern has |V1|-fold symmetry: V1 = -n gives n broad lobes with small loops - for example, V1 = -5 gives 5 lobes. V1 = n gives an n-point star - for example, V1 = 5 gives a 5-point star. V1 = 2 gives an exact ellipse: half its length is L1 + L2, half its width L1 - L2. A fraction such as -4/3 makes a pattern that takes 3 turns to close. The third circle (L3, V2) adds smaller loops on top of the pattern. Bazley's Figures T. S. Bazley's Index to the Geometric Chuck (1875) shows over 3,400 numbered figures, with the chuck settings for each. To cut one: Type the figure's number in Bazley figure . Type its size - the figure's largest diameter. Press Fill in . The chuck settings are set to cut that figure at that size, and the note under the drawings names the figure and its companion. The numbers are: 1 to 200 - Bazley's one-stage figures. 220 to 3454 - his two-stage figures. 3501 to 3554 - simple rose patterns (L1 and L2 the same), added by Roy Lindley. Bazley's figures come in companions - pairs with the same settings, except that the third circle starts half a turn round (G3 = 0 or 180). They are numbered one after the other, and look quite different. If you change a setting afterwards (other than Rotation), the pattern is no longer Bazley's figure, and the note no longer names it. Note: The settings for Bazley's figures are from Roy Lindley's catalogue (see Acknowledgement , above). Stages With Set by at Stages (r, k) , there are 5 circles, entered one by one. Each turns a circle of radius r , k times for each turn of the first, set at its Phase to start. Leave r at 0 for a stage you don't use. Use it for patterns a two-part chuck can't make - for example, four or five circles. r The stage's offset - the radius of its circle. k How many times the stage turns for each turn of the first: a whole number (6), a negative number for the other way (-4), or a fraction (7/3). Stage 1 is normally k = 1. Phase Where the stage is set at the start, in degrees. Switching back to Chuck settings keeps the pattern when a two-part chuck can make it. When it can't - more than three circles, stage 1 not at k = 1, or stages 1 and 2 at different phases - the Designer asks first, and the chuck settings replace the stages. Movement Cutting speed How fast the cutter moves along the pattern, in inches (or mm) per minute. C slows down where it, or X, can't keep up - most where the pattern passes close to the centre, where C has to turn fast. The note under the drawings gives the time. C direction Which way C turns: + or - . The other way cuts the pattern's mirror image. Depth Choose one: Passes, deeper each time The program cuts the pattern several times, going Depth per pass deeper before each pass, at the Plunge feed . The first pass is already one step deep, so put the cutter just touching the work. At the end, the cutter backs out to where it started. Finishing pass : tick it to run the last pass slower, for a better finish - at a percentage of the Cutting speed (10% to start). The earlier passes run at full speed. Spring passes : 0 to 5 extra times round at the final depth, to clean up the cut. They run at the finishing speed when Finishing pass is ticked. Times at one depth The program cuts the pattern the chosen number of times without going deeper. To go deeper, move the cutter in yourself and run the program again. Deeper is + : normally the cutter goes deeper by moving Z in its minus direction. Tick this if going deeper means moving it in its plus direction. Advance and Retract Cutter To start the cutter clear of the work, set how far it moves in before cutting - X advance , Z advance or both - and which way: Towards centre or Away for X, Towards headstock or Away for Z. Leave both at 0 for no advance. Put the cutter that far clear of where the pattern is to be cut. Run the program. The cutter moves in at the Plunge feed - Z last, so it enters the work straight in. The passes are cut from there - their depth is measured from where the advance ends. At the end, the cutter moves back out to exactly where you put it, Z first. Do not run the program until you have checked each advance's direction. The cutter moves the whole advance distance that way before it starts cutting - set the wrong way, it moves into the work instead of up to it. Note: If the program is stopped part way, the cutter is left where it stopped - it moves back out only at the normal end. Notes Under the Drawings Under the drawings, the Geometric Chuck Designer gives: the pattern's symmetry, and how many times one pattern turns C; its largest and smallest radius; how long one pattern and the whole program will take (counting a slower finishing pass); the radius to start the cutter at. for a Bazley figure, its number and its companion. It warns you when: the pattern passes through the centre, and how far X moves past C's axis (see below); C or X can't keep up at the cutting speed, so the cut slows down where it must; C, X or Z isn't assigned to a channel in Axis Selection - the program won't run until it is; a V or a k isn't a number. Setting the Start Radius: Check Circle The program moves the cutter from wherever it is put, so the cutter's distance from C's axis - the start radius the note under the drawings gives - sets the pattern's size and shape. That distance is hard to measure, especially when the headstock moves. The Check Circle... button makes it easy: it saves a small program that turns C once with X and Z standing still, so the cutter draws a circle round C's axis. The circle's diameter is twice the cutter's radius. Press Check Circle... . It saves a program named for the size the circle should be, for example Check-Circle_2.500in.ngc , in the gcode folder, and offers to open it in Rosetta. Put the cutter just touching the work, where the cut will start. Run the check circle program, and measure the circle's diameter. If the circle is smaller than it should be, move X out by half the difference; if it is bigger, move X in. For example, if a circle that should be 3\" across comes out 2 1/8\", move X out by 7/16\". Run the check circle again, until it is the right size. Open the pattern program and run it, without moving X. Note: with an X advance , set the circle first, then move X by the advance, as the check circle's message says, before running the pattern. Note: the check circle cuts a light circle in the work. On a paper chuck that doesn't matter; on finished work, set the radius on a scrap piece first. Note: the check circle works on any machine - see Working from Where the Cutter Starts in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge. Saving and Running the Program Press Save G-code... . The Geometric Chuck Designer suggests a name, such as GeoChuck-5fold_k6_face.ngc , in the gcode folder. If Rosetta is running, the Geometric Chuck Designer offers to open the program in Rosetta. Otherwise, open it in Rosetta with File > Open . Index C to turn the pattern where you want it on the work - the red dot on the drawing is where the cut starts. Put the cutter at the radius the note under the drawings gives, measured from the centre of C - use the check circle . Do not start the program until the cutter is at that radius. The program moves the cutter from wherever it is put, so started anywhere else the pattern comes out a different shape. Run the program. Do not run a pattern that passes through the centre until you have checked that X can travel past C's axis by the distance the note gives. To cut through the centre, the cutter crosses to the other side of the axis. If X can't travel that far, make the pattern smaller (smaller L's, a smaller Bazley size, or smaller r's). Note: while the program runs, the progress bar on the Rosetta panel shows which pass it is on, for example Program 42% - pass 2 of 3 . Do not name the program starting with \"Rosetta\", and do not name it M100 through M199. Those names are reserved (see Running Gcode Programs ), and the Geometric Chuck Designer refuses them. Note: C is not turned back at the end, so after several passes C may read thousands of degrees. The work is in the same place, and the next program again works from wherever C is. Changing a Saved Pattern Press Open... and choose a program the Geometric Chuck Designer saved. Its settings come back, ready to change and save again. The Geometric Chuck Designer also remembers every setting of the last pattern you worked on, so it opens the way you left it. Press New to start again from the standard pattern. Note: only programs saved by the Geometric Chuck Designer can be opened this way."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/Help.html","m":"User Manual","t":"ℛ Help","h":[],"x":"The ℛ Help tab on Rosetta’s main screen gathers the manuals and websites you may need while using Rosetta. Click a book to open it in the web browser. The User Manual , the Gcode Library and the Ornamental Turner’s Handy Book are installed on your Rosetta, so they open without an Internet connection, and each update brings their latest version. The others are websites and need an Internet connection; if your Rosetta isn’t connected, use another computer or a phone. Rosetta Library (the Rosetta banner) – the Rosetta website, rosetta.colvintools.com , with all of Rosetta’s documentation. Needs the Internet. User Manual – this manual: how to set up, configure and use Rosetta. On your Rosetta. Also opened by the Rosetta User Manual icon on the desktop and in Applications > Rosetta . Gcode Library – Gcode programs for the ornamental turner, each explained, ready to download into your Gcode folder. On your Rosetta. Also opened by the Rosetta Gcode Library icon on the desktop and in Applications > Rosetta . Gcode Quick Reference – LinuxCNC’s list of Gcode commands , for writing or changing your own programs. Needs the Internet. User Forum – the forum for questions and for sharing ideas with other Rosetta owners. Needs the Internet. Ornamental Turner’s Handy Book – a short reference (PDF) for the ornamental turner. On your Rosetta."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/HomeAllAxes.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Home All Axes (Ctrl-Home)","h":[],"x":"To home all axes, there are three options: Press the Ctrl and Home keys simultaneously on the keyboard Select Machine | Homing | Home All Axes from the menu bar. Use Home All Axes on the F3 Manual Control / Jogging screen To home an individual axis, Select Machine | Homing | Home Axis (axis) Rosetta does not use homing switches, so homing an axis simply sets its current position as its home (zero) position. Position the axis where you want home to be before homing it. (Limit switches are not used for homing.)"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/HowAllThePartsFitTogether.html","m":"User Manual","t":"How the Rosetta Programs Fit Together","h":[["Programs-on-the-System","Programs on the System"],["Manuals-on-the-Raspberry-Pi-and-the-Internet","Manuals on the Raspberry Pi and the Internet"],["Websites-on-the-Internet","Websites on the Internet"],["How-Base-LinuxCNC-is-Different-from-Rosetta","How Base LinuxCNC is Different from Rosetta"],["More-Than-a-Few-Add-ons","More Than a Few Add-ons"],["And-Some-Bases-to-Help-Users","And Some Bases to Help Users"]],"x":"Rosetta is not one program; instead, on the Rosetta system box(es), there are six programs on the machine which work together. Each one has its own job, its own icon, and its own entry on the Applications menu (click on Applications , then the arrow to the right of Rosetta). Rosetta and Rosetta Settings , the two you use every day, also have icons on the desktop. So do the Rosetta User Manual and Rosetta Gcode Library (see Manuals on the Raspberry Pi and the Internet , below). The six programs are related, because they all work on the same machine and share the same settings, but they are independent: each one is started on its own, and none of them starts the others. Knowing which program does what is the key to finding your way around the system. For example, if you are looking for a setting and cannot find it on the Rosetta screen, it is almost certainly in the Rosetta Settings program. Programs on the System Rosetta Rosetta Settings Update Rosetta The Turk's head The gear The green up arrow What it is: the machine control. It starts LinuxCNC with the Rosetta screens. What it is: the settings program. It tells Rosetta what is connected to the box, and how each device should behave. What it is: the software updater. Use it to: Jog the axes Run and index the spindles Index an axis Synchronize movement on multiple axes Perform a threading operation Run Gcode programs , including ones from the Gcode Library Use it to: Choose which axis or spindle is on each plug ( Axis Selection ) Maintain axis and stepper motor configuration settings Setup & maintain device-specific settings (e.g., different settings for a curvilinear slide vs. what would be used for the cross slide) Set up limit switches, the E-stop button, and the signal tower Set your desired measurement system (metric or Imperial) Configure other options to set the way the system works There are links on many pages in this program to the relevant pages of this User Manual. This is meant to help you understand how to best set the values to achieve what you want. Use it to: Update all the system software on Rosetta. This includes: Updates to the Rosetta code Any changes and updates to the config files within Rosetta Updates to LinuxCNC Updates to the operating system ( Debian Linux ) Updates to the virus scanning system ( ClamAV ) Backup Rosetta Rosette Designer Geometric Chuck Designer The USB Flash Drive The Rosette The GeoChuck Design What it is: a program for copying key user files to a USB flash drive . What it is: a program for designing rosettes in software to test them. What it is: a program for implementing a geometric chuck using software and stepper motors (usually attached to the cross slide). Use it to: Save all your key files just in case something crashes. There is an option to restore these files in the Rosetta Settings program. Use it to: Try a rosette before buying or making one. Cut with a rosette you would need too rarely to be worth making - the program is a software-defined rosette. Save the rosette design for 3D printing, or machining. Use it to: Try a geometric chuck pattern before cutting it. Cut geometric chuck patterns without a geometric chuck - the program is a software-defined geometric chuck. Shut Down - used to shut the computer down properly before you switch the power off. (See also, Switching the System Off .) Manuals on the Raspberry Pi and the Internet These two manuals are installed on your Rosetta, so they work without an Internet connection, and each update of the Rosetta software brings their latest version. Open them with the Rosetta User Manual and Rosetta Gcode Library icons on the desktop, from Applications > Rosetta , or from the Help tab on Rosetta’s main screen. The same pages are on the Rosetta website. User Manual - the documentation you are now viewing (on the Internet: https://rosetta.colvintools.com/UserManual ). Gcode Library - a library of Gcode programs which should be useful to the ornamental turner (on the Internet: https://rosetta.colvintools.com/Gcode/ ). These have been vetted and documented to assist you in using them. To use one of the Gcode programs, save it to your gcode library (~/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode). Some of these programs you will run as is; others you might modify or use as the basis for building your own Gcode programs. The online LinuxCNC Gcode Quick Reference is good if you do decide to make your own Gcode program. Websites on the Internet Rosetta Library These need an Internet connection. If your Rosetta isn’t connected, use another computer or a phone. Rosetta Library - the website for documentation about Rosetta ( https://rosetta.colvintools.com ). The manuals above, the forum and other references are all reached from this site. User Forum - a forum for exchanging information with other users of Rosetta ( https://colvintools.com/Rosetta/Forum/index.php ). How Base LinuxCNC is Different from Rosetta Rosetta is based on LinuxCNC with the Axis GUI . To that, we added programs and configurations which make LinuxCNC more useful for the ornamental turner. Graphically, you can see the difference in the images below. Base LinuxCNC using the Axis GUI Rosetta (with the Expansion Box) Debian Linux - Both systems run on Debian Linux which is a very solid version of Linux, and which also allows for operation as a real-time operating system . Debian is an open-source software operating system developed and maintained by the Debian Project. LinuxCNC - LinuxCNC (initially called Enhanced Machine Controller, or EMC2) was built by the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology in 1993 to reduce the cost for implementing an automation system for manufacturing. LinuxCNC implements a very powerful, standardized version of Gcode. The standard, RS274/NGC, is also defined by the International Standard, ISO 6983-1:2009. This enables you to develop your own Gcode programs and execute them on LinuxCNC. LinuxCNC is now an open-source software application which continues to be maintained and upgraded by a community of devotees. More Than a Few Add-ons LinuxCNC’s Tux (left) and Rosetta (right) But there is more to it than that. We have tried to provide an environment for using the system which makes it easy for you to make great art. Debian Linux Code Updates - And as with other computer operating systems, updates are needed to Debian to keep it running. Updates to it can be made using the standard means (sudo apt update / sudo apt upgrade); however, the Rosetta Upgrade program (the big green arrow) issues these commands for you. LinuxCNC Code Updates - also has updates over time. LinuxCNC updates are provided using the standard means (sudo apt update / sudo apt upgrade); however, the Rosetta Upgrade program (the big green arrow) issues these commands for you. Virus Scanner Updates - LinuxCNC is considered very secure; however there are many bad players out there on the internet. We have chosen to add ClamAV to the Rosetta system as an extra measure of security. (This can be added to other LinuxCNC systems but is not by default.) ClamAV is an open-source software application which continues to be maintained and upgraded by a community of devotees. Updates to the application are provided using standard means (sudo apt update / sudo apt upgrade / freshclam); however, the Rosetta Upgrade program (the big green arrow) issues these commands for you. Fixes to the Update Process - As with many operating systems, there are occasional issues encountered with the upgrade process (sudo apt update / sudo apt upgrade). When we encounter these, we add the fix for that issue into the Rosetta Upgrade program (the big green arrow) so that you do not have to research the needed actions. It may require you to run the Upgrade program twice, but the issue will be resolved for you. Rosetta Code Updates - We expect to provide updates over time. The Rosetta Upgrade program (the big green arrow) will pull these down to your system, and will make any updates to the on-board files for you. And Some Bases to Help Users Rosetta User Manual - the on-line user manual for Rosetta is provided for helping users of the system. Rosetta Support Manual - the on-line support manual for Rosetta is where we document a lot of technical details to keep those documents readily at hand. Rosetta User Forum - the on-line user forum for Rosetta is provided for helping users of the system. Rosetta Preconfigured Hardware - Being able to purchase an off-the-shelf system which you can plug in and immediately start using is a great option for the ornamental turner who does not want to get into building their own system. We worked to select hardware which meets the application needs, can be sourced at reasonable costs, and can be provided by vendors reliably over time. A number of products were tested which did not meet our expectations, but in the end, we were able to identify some good options. We also developed a customized printed circuit board (PCB) which makes the wiring far easier (reducing the cost) and also makes the system more reliable. Rosetta Cabling Standards - The standards for cabling we have selected help to reduce problems for the user, and eliminate the need to do your own research. The cable ends were selected to provide: Ease of use Secure connections (so the cables do not get accidentally disconnected) Sizes and types which ensure you do not plug each device into the wrong jack Rosetta Configuration Application - A rose engine's user needs the ability to reconfigure the system as necessary for the art to be produced. These changes can necessitate changes to the way LinuxCNC is configured. Traditionally, that was done by editing a text file, and requires a good knowledge of what to change (and what to not change). The Rosetta Settings program (the big gear) makes all this much easier, and has protections built in to help ensure you do not make settings which would make your system work poorly (or not work). Rosetta Backup Application - There are a number of files you would probably hate to lose: these include your current settings, and any Gcode programs you are using. This program makes it easy to copy those files to a USB flash drive . LinuxCNC User Manual - the on-line user manual for LinuxCNC is very good. It applies to the base system, and we doubt you will ever need it, but it can be useful for the Rosetta user also. LinuxCNC User Forum - the on-line user forum for LinuxCNC is also very good. It applies to the base system, and we doubt you will ever need it, but it can be useful for the Rosetta user also."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/InputsOutputsSound.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds","h":[["Limit-Switches","Limit Switches"],["Assign-a-Limit-Switch-to-an-Axis","Assign a Limit Switch to an Axis"],["Limit-Switch-Types","Limit Switch Types"],["Switch-Configuration","Switch Configuration"],["Pause-Button","Pause Button"],["E-Stop-Button","E-Stop Button"],["Signal-Tower","Signal Tower"],["Sounds_","Sounds"],["Phone-Alerts","Phone Alerts"],["Setting-Up-Phone-Alerts","Setting Up Phone Alerts"],["What-Is-Sent","What Is Sent"],["Running-the-Rosetta-Settings-Program","Running the Rosetta Settings Program"],["Option-1-Use-the-icon-on-the-desktop","Option 1: Use the icon on the desktop"],["Option-2-Use-the-Applications-menu","Option 2: Use the Applications menu"]],"x":"Key Note: The configuration program cannot be run when the Rosetta program is running. It is designed so that you configure the way Rosetta works, then run the system. Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds Limit Switches When a limit switch is tripped, LinuxCNC turns the machine off. Assign a Limit Switch to an Axis To use a limit switch, you need to assign it to an axis. Rosetta is configured to accommodate 6 limit switches: Limit Jacks 1 - 3 are on the Base Box, and Limit Jacks 4 - 6 are on the optional Expansion Box. These can only be used when the Expansion Box is ticked as Connected on the Axis Selection tab. Otherwise they are greyed out; they keep their setting, ready for when the Expansion Box is connected. Each Limit Jack only lists the axes which are assigned to a channel. By default, all limit jacks are not enabled. Limit Switch Types The limit switches default to normally closed (NC), but the user can change them to be normally open (NO). Normally closed is strongly recommended - see Limit Switch in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge for why, and for more on wiring and placing limit switches. NC The axis to which a limit switch is assigned will be in a limit mode until a switch is connected to the system, and that switch is not triggered. NO The axis to which a limit switch is assigned will not be in a limit mode until a switch is connected to the system, and that switch is triggered. Note : The axis to which a limit switch is assigned will not be in a limit mode if there is a break in the wire. Thusly, you should test to ensure it works before expecting it to (and find out it does not). Switch Configuration Limit switches can be configured these ways. Both ends (for a selected axis) This will enable the user to connect two or more switches for the same axis, and either switch can trigger a limit. For NC connections, the switches would be wired in series, and if any of those breaks the circuit, the limit is triggered for that axis. For NO connections, the switches would be wired in parallel, and if any of those closes the circuit, the limit is triggered for that axis. With this configuration, when an axis’ limit switch is triggered, LinuxCNC can't tell which end was triggered. Thusly, you will need to power the machine back on (F2), then check Override Limits (in LinuxCNC's Machine menu) to jog movement on this axis to disengage the switch. Min end (for a selected axis) Min end (for a selected axis): This will trigger a limit when movement hits the minimum movement. By convention, this is in the negative side of movement. For example, on the Z axis, this would be on the left side. With this configuration, when an axis’ limit switch is triggered, LinuxCNC will allow you to power the machine back on (F2), then jog movement on this axis away from the limit switch position (e.g., in the positive direction). Max end (for a selected axis) Max end (for a selected axis): This will trigger a limit when movement hits the maximum movement. By convention, this is in the positive side of movement. For example, on the Z axis, this would be on the right side. With this configuration, when an axis’ limit switch is triggered, LinuxCNC will allow you to power the machine back on (F2), then jog movement on this axis away from the limit switch position (e.g., in the negative direction). Pause button For any jack used for the Pause button instead of a limit switch. Not used For any jack with nothing plugged into it. For NC connections, this is critical if no switch it is to be used. Otherwise, there will be an open circuit and a limit will be triggered for that axis. For any axis, the limit switches can be configured for that axis with: One both ends jack, or One min jack, or One max jack, or One min and one max jack, and One pause button Notes : Spindles using the Sp0 or Sp1 connections cannot have limit switches assigned to them. Cabling for the limit switches . Pause Button Instead of a limit switch, one limit jack may be used as a Pause button (i.e., a push button you can reach quickly at the machine). To use a pause button, set the jack you wish to use to Pause button . Only one jack can be the Pause button; choosing it on a second jack is flagged as Duplicate! and is not saved until it is corrected. Pressing the Pause button pauses: a Sync Move - carry on with Continue on the main screen, or use Return to Start (see Pausing a Sync Move ), or a G-code program - carry on with LinuxCNC's Resume button, or with Continue on the main screen's Sync Move tab. The Pause button only pauses. Pressing it again does not resume the operation: that is always done using a button on the screen. It does not pause Cut Thread or indexing. Using the pause button does not drive a fault: there is no red light or siren as there is with the use of an E-stop. The Pause button uses the same Limit Switch Type as the limit switches: with NC (normally closed), use a normally closed push button. with NO (normally open), use a normally open push button. Do not plug in a push button of the other type. It would read as pressed all the time. The pause button is engaged: When the button reads as pressed When the circuit is configured as NC and: a NO button is plugged into the circuit, or there is a broken wire When the circuit is configured as NO and: a NC button is plugged into the circuit When a pause button is engaged, Sync Move and LinuxCNC's Run will not start. The system will note that the Pause button is pressed or disconnected. Notes : The Pause button is wired like a limit switch - see Limit Switch Cabling . E-Stop Button For the E-Stop button, this is either set as connected, or not. The E-Stop button plugs into the Base Box. The E-Stop button is defined as a normally closed (NC) circuit, so once the circuit encounters a break (i.e., when the E-Stop button is engaged), the system will move to an Emergency Stop status. Key Note : As this is a normally closed circuit, once the use of an E-Stop button is enabled, the system cannot be taken out of Emergency Stop until a button is attached to the system and it is in a disengaged state. Note : Cabling for the E-Stop button Usage instructions for the E-Stop button The E-Stop button cannot be setup to be normally open. Signal Tower The signal tower plugs into the Base Box. It is setup in Rosetta to have 3 outputs: RED LIGHT : A signal which lights a red light when: A following error is encountered on any active axis. The red light stays lit until the machine is turned back on (F2). A limit switch is tripped on any in-use limit jack. The red light stays lit until the limit switch is no longer tripped. The Base Box or Expansion Box loses contact with the Raspberry Pi (see Lost contact ). It does not light for: E-stop - pressing the E-Stop button or F1 Program problems: Gcode errors Programs which are stopped or aborted Normal Machine off - turning the machine off (F2) GREEN LIGHT : A signal which lights a green light to indicate that the system is running an operation: Running one of the defined operations in the operator panel Running a Gcode command Running an MDI command While a running program is paused It does not light for: Jogging When the system is idle When the system has finished an operation (or the operation was aborted) When the machine is in either Emergency Stop status, or is in a Powered Off status BUZZER : A buzzer is set to sound for certain situations. Those are outlined below in sounds . Note : Cabling for the signal tower . Sounds Most sounds are available to heard on either of two output devices, or both: Signal tower’s siren, Monitor’s speaker (via HDMI), or Both There is one sound which cannot be played on the signal tower’s siren: that is the one alerting the user that a reserved name has been used for a gcode program in the gcode directory. That is because this error is identified and sounded before LinuxCNC is started. More information about sounds . Phone Alerts Rosetta can send a message to your phone when something happens at the machine, so you can leave it while it works. The messages come through ntfy , a free app for the iPhone (App Store) and Android phones (Google Play). Setting Up Phone Alerts Install the ntfy app on your phone. In Rosetta Settings, on the Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab, check Send alerts to my phone . The ntfy topic box is filled in with Rosetta- and your machine's serial number, for example Rosetta-Rosetta003 . In the ntfy app, tap + , type in the topic exactly as Rosetta Settings shows it (capital letters matter), and tap Subscribe . In Rosetta Settings, press Send Test Message . It shows Sent - check your phone , and the test message arrives on your phone within a few seconds. Under Send When , tick the events you want to be told about. Start Rosetta. Phone alerts take effect the next time Rosetta starts. What Is Sent Each message's title is Rosetta, S/N and your machine's serial number, so you can tell which machine sent it. Finished A G-code program, a Sync Move or a Cut Thread finished. For example: Program finished (Geo1216.ngc). Nothing is sent when you stop one part way. Paused (M0/M1) A G-code program stopped at an M0 or M1 to wait for you. For example: Program paused at line 42 (Geo1216.ngc) - waiting for you. Fault siren A following error or a tripped limit switch stopped the machine - the same time the fault siren sounds and the signal tower's red light comes on. Sent as urgent. Pi too hot The Raspberry Pi reached its Alarm temperature (see Pi Temperature ). Box lost contact The Base Box or the Expansion Box lost contact with the Raspberry Pi, and the machine is in E-stop (see Lost contact ). Sent as urgent. Do not rely on phone alerts to keep the machine safe. They are a convenience. If the Raspberry Pi can't reach the internet, or your phone has no signal, the message is lost and is not sent again. Never leave a running machine where you couldn't get back to it quickly. Do not give your topic to anyone you don't want reading your alerts. Anyone who knows the topic can subscribe to it in the ntfy app and see your messages. To make it harder to guess, add a word of your own to the end of the topic in Rosetta Settings, for example Rosetta-Rosetta003-bluebird , then subscribe to that same topic in the ntfy app . Notes : Phone alerts are sent whatever the Sounds are set to. For example, with the Fault Siren set to Off , the Fault siren alert is still sent. If you have more than one Rosetta, subscribe to each machine's topic in the ntfy app. The Raspberry Pi needs the internet for phone alerts - see Setting up Wi-Fi . If an alert doesn't arrive, see Phone alerts don't arrive . Running the Rosetta Settings Program To access the Rosetta Settings program, there are 2 options: Option 1: Use the icon on the desktop Double-click on the Rosetta Settings icon on the desktop (the big gear) Option 2: Use the Applications menu Click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen Click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta Click on Rosetta Settings (the one with a gear) Applications Rosetta > Rosetta Rosetta Settings Backup Rosetta Update Rosetta Geometric Chuck Designer Rosette Designer"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/LimitSwitchCabling.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Cabling Limit Switches or a Pause Button to Rosetta","h":[["Connector","Connector"],["Cable-Sizing","Cable Sizing"],["Wiring-the-Switches","Wiring the Switches"],["Limit-Switch-Notes","Limit Switch Notes"]],"x":"GX-12/2 Plug Connector A GX-12/2 female plug is used to connect limit switches or a pause button to the Rosetta system. This plug is sometimes called an aviation connector . Cable Sizing The cable between the Rosetta system and your limit switch or pause button should: Have 2 conductors within the cable Be stranded - this is more flexible than solid wire Be 22 AWG (gauge; 24 AWG is the minimum) - this is easily soldered to the GX-12 connector Be shielded, with the shield connected to ground at the Rosetta box end only - this will help isolate the signal from noise which could be encountered in adjacent cables (e.g., for stepper motors) Wiring the Switches GX-12 Pin Wire Color Purpose 1 Red Signal (to the Rosetta input) - do not connect to any power source 2 Black GND (ground) Limit Switch Notes Limit switches are typically wired as normally closed (NC), and the circuit becomes open when the switch is triggered. If you wire them as normally open (NO), there is a setting in the configuration program you will need to use to make this work as you expect. The switch simply connects the two pins together when it is not triggered. The Rosetta box supplies the signal itself, so never connect either wire to a power supply. Each jack takes either: one switch, for one end of an axis's travel (set the jack to min or max ), or two switches wired in series, one at each end (set the jack to both ends ). To protect both ends of an axis and still let LinuxCNC tell which end was hit (for example, each end of a curvilinear slide), use two jacks - one set to min and one to max . A limit jack can take an external Pause button instead of a limit switch, wired the same way - see Pause Button . Route limit switch cables away from stepper motor cables where you can, and cross them at right angles rather than bundling them together, so electrical noise doesn't cause false trips. The use of such a switch must be configured on the Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab of the Rosetta Settings program."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/MDITab.html","m":"User Manual","t":"MDI Tab (F5)","h":[["Running-Gcode-Commands","Running Gcode Commands"],["Jog-Speed-Sliders","Jog Speed Sliders"],["Modal-Gcodes-in-Effect","Modal Gcodes in Effect"],["Accessing-this-Screen","Accessing this Screen"]],"x":"Running Gcode Commands This is where gcode program commands can be entered manually, one line at a time. In the picture to the right, the commands are entered in the field about ⅔ of the way down the screen, and has the title above it, MDI Command . After you enter the gcode command you want to run, click Go . Commands you have run before are shown in the History . The latest command run is at the bottom of the list. If you click on any entry, it will copy that entry into the MDI Command field. Then you can click Go to run that command. (You cannot double-click on an entry in the history and get it executed. You have to click on it, and then click Go .) Jog Speed Sliders The sliders below that field are used to override the values for the Manual Control / Jogging screen. Modal Gcodes in Effect Finally, this page also shows which modal G-codes are in effect. This is in the Active G-Codes: field at the bottom of the page. See also, Gcode Quick Reference . Accessing this Screen You can either click on the tab, or use the F5 key to access this."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/MainScreenIndexing.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Indexing","h":[["Axes-Colors","Axes Colors"],["Indexing-Tab","Indexing Tab"],["Counter","Counter"],["Spindles","Spindles"],["Linear-Axes","Linear Axes"],["Angular-Rotary-Axes","Angular (Rotary) Axes"]],"x":"Axes Colors Color Axis Movement Control Neg Pos Neg Pos Yellow X U A Red V B Blue Z W C Black Spindles (Sp0 & Sp1) On this page, the axis letters are colored to assist the user. (Note: LinuxCNC does not allow for a Y axis when running as a lathe.) Every axis has a row of controls on the user screens, but only the axes selected for use can be operated - the others are grayed out. For example, in the screen shown below, the user has selected to use the X, Z, U, V, W, and C axes, so the A and B rows are grayed out. The table at the bottom of the user screen titled, Axis Assignments , shows the current axis assignments. It is updated the next time Rosetta is started after the selected axes are changed. Indexing Tab The Indexing tab is used to drive motors on the selected axis, one at a time. The buttons to the right initiate the indexing operation in either the reverse (- movement) or forward (+ movement) direction. Images on the buttons show the movement direction. To index an axis, follow these steps: Step Action Value Saved? 1 The axis must be ENAbled. The ENAble status for an axis is not saved when exiting Rosetta. It will always default to not enabled. If it is not enabled when you index, Rosetta asks whether to enable it (see ENA ). No 2 The feed rate for the axis must be a non-zero number. Yes 3 The distance to move the axis must be a non-zero number. Yes 4 To initiate the movement, click on the button showing the direction for the desired movement. No Counter There is a counter for the number of indexing operations which have been performed. This count is shown as the number to the left of the indexing Distance . It is incremented by 1 for each Fwd indexing operation. It is decremented by 1 for each Rev indexing operation. The count starts at 0 when Rosetta is started. ( It is not saved when you exit and then re-start Rosetta. ) If the indexing Distance is changed, it reverts to 0. (T he assumption is that you are starting a different activity. ) You can set it back to 0 yourself by double-clicking on the number ( double-clicking is implemented so you do not accidentally reset it ). Spindles For the spindles, this can be in either degrees or divisions of a circle. Additionally, you can choose to index: the main spindle (Sp0), the rosette phaser multiplier (Sp1), or both If neither is selected, nothing happens. A spindle index always turns in the direction of the button you click , for the full distance, even when going the other way round would be shorter. For example, a Fwd index of 270 degrees turns the spindle 270 degrees forward; it does not turn it 90 degrees in reverse. An index of 180 degrees (2 divisions) also turns the same way every time. The reason is backlash - the small amount of free play in the gears or belt between the motor and the spindle. Each time the spindle changes direction, the motor has to take up that play before the spindle starts to move, so an index which reversed would stop short by the amount of backlash. Turning the same way for every index keeps the index positions evenly spaced. Do not mix Fwd and Rev indexes while you are working around a pattern. Click the same direction button for every index of the pattern, so the backlash is always taken up the same way. Do not back up one index with a single click of the other direction button. The spindle would stop short by the amount of backlash. If you go one index too far, click the other direction button twice , then click the original direction button once - the spindle then arrives at the position from the usual direction. Note: Each index is measured from where the previous index was meant to stop, not from where the spindle actually stopped, so the tiny differences in where it stops do not add up as you work around the circle. For example, 24 divisions done all the way round end back where they started. After the spindle has been run (not indexed), the next index starts from wherever the spindle is. Linear Axes For linear axes, this is a distance. The UOM shown for these axes, and used by the system, is based on the Measurement System selection. Angular (Rotary) Axes For the angular axes (A, B, and C), this is a rotary indexing, in degrees or divisions of a circle."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/MainScreenSynchronizedMovement.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Synchronized Movement","h":[["Axes-Colors","Axes Colors"],["Synchronized-Movement-Tab","Synchronized Movement Tab"],["Linear-Axes","Linear Axes"],["Angular-Axes","Angular Axes"],["Using-the-Sync-Move-Tab","Using the Sync Move Tab"],["Pausing-a-Sync-Move","Pausing a Sync Move"]],"x":"Axes Colors Color Axis Movement Control Neg Pos Neg Pos Yellow X U A Red V B Blue Z W C Black Spindles (Sp0 & Sp1) On this page, the axis letters are colored to assist the user. (Note: LinuxCNC does not allow for a Y axis when running as a lathe.) Every axis has a row of controls on the user screens, but only the axes selected for use can be operated - the others are grayed out. For example, in the screen shown below, the user has selected to use the X, Z, U, V, W, and C axes, so the A and B rows are grayed out. The table at the bottom of the user screen titled, Axis Assignments , shows the current axis assignments. It is updated the next time Rosetta is started after the selected axes are changed. Synchronized Movement Tab The Sync Move tab is used to drive synchronized movement of multiple axes. Linear Axes For linear axes (X, Z, U, V, and W), this is a distance. The UOM shown for these axes, and used by the system, is based on the Measurement System selection. Angular Axes For the angular axes (A, B, and C), this is a rotary indexing. Beside each distance, choose its unit: deg - degrees, with 2 decimal places, up to 99,999,999.00 degrees. rev - revolutions (full turns), with 3 decimal places, up to 999,999.999 revolutions. One revolution is 360 degrees, so 40 revolutions can be entered as 40 instead of 14,400 degrees. You can type a distance with or without commas (1000000 and 1,000,000 are the same). Once you press Enter, the field shows the number with commas. Notes : When you change between deg and rev , the number in the field stays the same and its meaning changes - 40 degrees becomes 40 revolutions (and vice versa). Check the number after changing the unit. Revolutions are acceptable to use when the fraction can be expressed in up to 3 decimal places. ½ = 0.5, ¼ = 0.25, and ⅛ = 0.125. When the fraction cannot be expressed in 3 decimal places (e.g., ⅓ or ⅙), degrees may be a better choice. 0.333 revolutions results in 119.88° of movement (not 120°), and 3 of these movements will not come back to the same start (3 x 119.88° = 359.64°). Key Note : With LinuxCNC, synchronized motion of the spindles cannot be achieved using Sp0 nor Sp1. Those motors must be attached to an angular axis (A, B, or C). It is a good idea to perform a \"dry run\" to ensure everything moves as expected. You may need to change the setup for the axes (e.g., the Scale ) to achieve your desired movements. Using the Sync Move Tab To drive synchronized movement on selected axes, follow these steps: Step Action Value Saved? 1 The axis must be ENAbled. (This can be done automatically if Axis Not Enabled is set to Enable it automatically .) The ENAble status for an axis is not saved when exiting Rosetta. It will always default to not enabled. No 2 The feed rate for each axis must be a non-zero, positive number. Note: all the selected axes start and finish together. The move takes as long as whichever axis needs the most time at its own feed rate, so no axis moves faster than the feed rate you set for it. Yes 3 The axes must be selected. In the picture above, the blue tick boxes indicate that the X, Z, & C axes have been selected for use. If an axis is ENAbled but not selected, then it will simply not run. Yes 4 The distance to move the axis must be a non-zero, positive number. For A, B, and C, also choose deg or rev (see Angular Axes above). Yes 5 A direction of movement for the axis must be selected. In the picture above, X is to move in the reverse direction. Z is to move in the forward direction. C is to move in the forward direction. Yes 6 To initiate the movement, click on the Sync Move button below the axis distance fields. To stop the movement part way, click on the Pause button (see Pausing a Sync Move below). To return the system to where it was at the start of this operation, click on the Return to Start button. n/a In the table above, where Value Saved? is Yes , that value is saved when exiting Rosetta, and will be the same when Rosetta is restarted. The deg / rev choice for each angular axis is saved too. Pausing a Sync Move The Pause button, between Sync Move and Return to Start , stops a Sync Move part way - for example, when the belt driving the cutter breaks, or when you see the cutter is going to hit the chuck. The axes slow down and stop where they are. The button then reads Continue , and you can either: click Continue to finish the move. The axes carry on along the same path, at the same speed, as if the move had not been paused, or click Return to Start to send the axes back to where the Sync Move started. The progress bar shows (paused) while the move is paused. Pausing does not stop the spindles, or a cutter driven by its own motor. You can turn the machine off, or press E-Stop, while the move is paused (for example, to replace a broken belt). When the machine is back on, Continue still finishes the move from where the axes stopped. Do not move an axis of a paused Sync Move (by jogging, indexing, or running a program) unless you mean to leave its path. Continue goes straight from wherever the axes are to the Sync Move's end point. If an axis was moved off the path, Rosetta says which one and asks before continuing; choose Return to Start instead if you are not sure the way is clear. Notes : Stopping a Sync Move with LinuxCNC's own Stop button (or the Esc key) also lets you use Continue or Return to Start afterwards. You can also pause a Sync Move with an external Pause button plugged into a limit jack - see Pause Button . The external button only pauses; use Continue on the screen to carry on. When a G-code program is paused (by LinuxCNC's Pause button, an M0 or M1 in the program, or the external Pause button), this button reads Continue too, and clicking it carries on with the program - the same as LinuxCNC's Resume button."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/MainScreenThreading.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Threading","h":[["Axes-Colors","Axes Colors"],["Threading-Tab","Threading Tab"],["Data-Entry","Data Entry"],["Cut-the-Threads","Cut the Threads"],["After-Cutting-the-Threads","After Cutting the Threads"]],"x":"Axes Colors Color Axis Movement Control Neg Pos Neg Pos Yellow X U A Red V B Blue Z W C Black Spindles (Sp0 & Sp1) On this page, the axis letters are colored to assist the user. (Note: LinuxCNC does not allow for a Y axis when running as a lathe.) Every axis has a row of controls on the user screens, but only the axes selected for use can be operated - the others are grayed out. For example, in the screen shown below, the user has selected to use the X, Z, U, V, W, and C axes, so the A and B rows are grayed out. The table at the bottom of the user screen titled, Axis Assignments , shows the current axis assignments. It is updated the next time Rosetta is started after the selected axes are changed. Threading Tab The Threading tab is used to cut threads with any number of starts. This uses a Rosetta threading routine which cuts the same passes as LinuxCNC's G76 threading cycle. (G76 itself cannot be used on Rosetta, because it expects the work to be turned by the Sp0 spindle, and here it is turned by the C axis.) It uses a multi-pass approach: the first pass cuts to the First cut depth , and each following pass goes that much deeper again, until the Final depth is reached. With more than one start, each start is cut to full depth before C turns to the next start. You can also set the number of spring passes. These are passes at full depth made after the thread has been fully cut, and are used to smooth out the cuts. You can also notice in this screen that I have reconfigured the spindle to be the C axis. Sp0 cannot be used for this operation. The axes used are: X, Z, & C. The cutter is driven by a drilling spindle. Data Entry Field Data Description Value Saved? Starts Select the number of starts you wish to use. 1 is typical, but it can be any integer value (though I wouldn't go too high). Yes TPI Select the number of starts you wish to use. 1 is typical, but it can be any integer value (though I wouldn't go too high). Yes Thread Length Enter the thread length. This is the distance you want threads to be placed onto the piece. Yes Thread Angle Enter the thread cutter’s angle. This is only used to calculate the maximum thread depth. 60° cutters are most commonly used, but 90° and even 120° cutters are also seen. Yes Handedness Select if the threads are right handed (standard) or left handed. Yes Inside or Outside Threads Select if you are cutting the threads on the inside of a piece, or the outside. This determines how the cutter is pulled away from the piece between passes. Yes Direction Select the direction of the cutting: left to right, or right to left. Yes Clearance Distance Set the clearance distance. This is how much the cutter is placed away from the piece before starting, and is used for pulling the cutter away from the piece when re-positioning it. Yes 1st Cut Depth Set the first cut depth. This should be driven by the material’s hardness. Yes Final Cut Depth Set the final cut depth. The maximum this can be is calculated based on the cutter’s thread angle. Yes Spring Passes Set the number of spring passes. These are passes made using the cutter at full cutter depth, and are used to clean up any cutting from the final cut depth pass. It is generally recommended that this not be too high as it can also damage the threads already made. Yes As you complete these entries, you will see a message just above the buttons at the bottom which identifies the total number of passes which will be made. Cut the Threads For this operation, C is used to rotate the piece being threaded (AKA, the spindle) Z moves the cutter through the cutting cycle X moves the cutter into / away from the cutting The steps are: Step Action 1 Ensure the Z axis moves in a way that is parallel to the spindle’s axis of rotation. 2 Install a thread cutter into the cutter holder (usually a drilling spindle), and ensure it is running in the correct direction. 3 Position the cutter at the start of the cut (Z axis). 4 Ensure the cutter is the distance away from the piece noted in the Clearance value (X axis). 5 ENAble the X, Z, and C axes. If any of them is not enabled when you click Cut Thread , Rosetta asks whether to enable it (see ENA ). 6 Once you click Cut Thread , everything will happen automagically. As the cuts are being made, a message will appear below these buttons which indicates which pass is being performed. Also, the image on the Preview screen will indicate what is happening. After Cutting the Threads You can click Return to Start if you want to reposition the cutter back to the beginning."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/ManualControl.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Manual Control / Jogging (F3)","h":[["Jog-an-individual-axes","Jog an individual axes"],["Home-All-Axes","Home All Axes"]],"x":"This screen allows the user to perform many functions. Jog an individual axes To do this, Select the axis to jog Ensure the selected axis is enabled Use the pull-down to select either Continuous movement or a select distance Use the - (minus) or + (plus) button to move the axis the selected distance. Note: The Sp1 (Rosette Phaser Multiplier) axis cannot be operated from this screen. This is a limitation with the Axis display in LinuxCNC. Home All Axes This button is used to Home All axes."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/NewSystemStartup.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Starting Up Your New System","h":[["Additional-Notes","Additional Notes"],["System-Usage-Notes","System Usage Notes"],["Changes-to-System-Settings","Changes to System Settings"],["Switching-the-System-Off","Switching the System Off"],["Entering-Data-Values","Entering Data Values"],["User-Settings-Files","User Settings Files"]],"x":"These are the steps for getting your new Rosetta system started up. Connect all the hardware together: Connect the display, keyboard, and mouse to the system. If you have an Expansion Box, plug its network cable and power into the Base Box. Connect the power cord to an outlet from the Base Box. Power on WiFi Icon the monitor, and the Base Box on (this will also power on the Expansion Box). Connect to Wifi - click on the Wi-Fi icon located on the title bar at the top of the system. It is the one indicated on the image to the right. Update the Rosetta software on your Rosetta system: click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen, then the arrow to the right of Rosetta, then Update Rosetta (the green arrow). Connect the stepper motors to the machine. If you need to add connectors to your cables, there is a wiring diagram in this user manual. If you are using limit switches , an E-stop button , or a signal tower , connect those too. Use the big gear on the desktop to run the Rosetta Settings program. Axis Selection tab Tick Expansion Box - Connected if you have one Choose which axis each plug is used for. Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab Set up limit switches, E-stop button, signal tower, and sounds. Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning tab Set the scales needed for the devices you are using. We recommend you leave the other settings at the default until you use the system a bit. Other tab Select the Measurement System you wish to use We recommend you leave the other settings at the default until you use the system a bit. Axis Selection tab Click Save All Settings , then close the program. Start Rosetta (double-click on the Turk's head icon), then press F1 to release the E-stop and F2 to power the machine on. Additional Notes If you run the update program too soon after starting up your system, you may encounter an error. This is because the virus scanning is still running. Wait 30 minutes and run it again. This should clear up any issues. System Usage Notes Changes to System Settings Settings are changed in the separate Rosetta Settings program, which cannot run while Rosetta is running. To change a setting: Close Rosetta by clicking on the X in the top right of the screen, and give it a minute or so to let the shutdown program complete. Run the Settings program, make your changes, and click Save All Settings on the Axis Selection tab. Close the Settings program, then double-click on the Turk's head icon to restart Rosetta. Switching the System Off Do not switch the power off while the computer is running. Switching off a running computer can damage the files on its memory card. Instead: Close Rosetta, and any other Rosetta program that is open. Click on Shut Down - at the bottom of the Applications menu, or the red power symbol on the bar that pops up at the bottom middle of the screen. Click Shut Down to confirm. If a Rosetta program is still open, it asks you to close it first. When the screen has gone dark, wait 10 seconds, then switch the power off. Entering Data Values The system used for building screens in LinuxCNC has some quirks. To change a value, you must have the mouse over that field. To have the system accept the value you entered, you must depress the Enter key after entering the value. System checks are built into many fields to help you verify that you are not entering invalid values. For example, there is a relationship between the axis scaling and the axis max speed. Thusly, we have the system ensure you do not enter a set of values which is too high (and which would not work correctly, thusly bringing up an error message). User Settings Files Your settings are saved in the file ~/Documents/RosettaSet_v1.ini The Settings program can also save a copy of all your settings, or just some of them, into another file, and load settings back from a file. See Saving and Sharing Settings ."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/Other.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Other Configuration Settings","h":[["Other-Tuning-Jog-Speeds","Other Tuning (Jog Speeds)"],["General","General"],["Device-Names","Device Names"],["Startup-File-Check","Startup File Check"],["Axis-Not-Enabled","Axis Not Enabled"],["Raspberry-Pi-Temperature","Raspberry Pi Temperature"],["Measurement-System","Measurement System"],["Running-the-Rosetta-Settings-Program","Running the Rosetta Settings Program"],["Option-1-Use-the-icon-on-the-desktop","Option 1: Use the icon on the desktop"],["Option-2-Use-the-Applications-menu","Option 2: Use the Applications menu"]],"x":"Key Note: The configuration program cannot be run when the Rosetta program is running. It is designed so that you configure the way Rosetta works, then run the system. Other Settings Other Tuning (Jog Speeds) These are the speed options which can be used on the Manual Control / Jogging (F3) screen. Use this to set the maximum, default, and minimum jogging speeds for Linear axes: Jog speed for the X, Z, U, V, & W axes. Rotary (Angular) axis: Jog speed for the A, B, & C axes. These are used when moving under Manual Control [F3]. Linear jog speeds are in inches (or mm) per second, and rotary jog speeds are in degrees per second. The jog speed slider in Rosetta shows the same speeds per minute - for example, a Default of 5.8333 deg/sec shows there as 350 deg/min. For each, the values must be in order: Minimum ≤ Default ≤ Maximum A change that would break that order is not accepted - the field goes back to its saved value and a message explains why. To move a value further, change the others first (for example, raise the Maximum before raising the Default above it). These changes are saved as soon as you make them, and take effect the next time Rosetta starts. General There are some general values which get set here. Device Names These get used for the selection fields on the main panel (to the right side). You enter them as one device name per line. When using Save Subset of Settings from the Axis Selection screen, you can pick one of these names to label which physical device the exported axis' values belong to. Startup File Check When Rosetta is started, it checks to determine if you are using a reserved file name for a gcode program’s name. This selection determines what happens if a reserved file name is found. Abort startup (the default) - LinuxCNC does not start until the file is renamed. This is the recommended approach as there are potential problems if a file with a reserved name is saved in the gcode directory. Warn, but let me continue - LinuxCNC starts anyway, once you close the warning. This is NOT recommended. Note: A short sound plays if startup is aborted - see Sounds . Axis Not Enabled This dictates what happens when an activity is meant to move an axis or spindle, and that axis is not enabled (i.e., the ENA button is off / red). Those activities include: using the index operation, using the synchronized movement, using the cut thread operation, or running a Gcode program (when you click Run), Without its ENA on, an axis stops with a Joint following error as soon as it has to move, and a spindle does not turn, so its index never finishes. The options available for these scenarios are: Ask (the default) - a pop-up box names the axes which are not enabled, and offers two buttons: Enable ... and Run (on the main screen, Enable ... and Move ) - turns those ENA buttons on, the same as clicking them, then carries on. This is the default button, so pressing Enter chooses it. Cancel - leaves them off, and nothing moves. Enable it automatically - the ENA buttons are turned on with no box, and Rosetta carries on. For a Gcode program, a short note in the bottom right corner of the LinuxCNC window says which axes were enabled. Notes: If the system is set to automatically enable an axis when needed, that axis cannot be enabled if it is not configured for use. See also, Axis Selection . This choice is saved as soon as you make it, and takes effect the next time Rosetta starts. Raspberry Pi Temperature The Raspberry Pi is the small computer inside the Base Box that runs Rosetta and LinuxCNC. Like any computer it gets warm while it works, and it has a fan to keep it cool. Its temperature is shown at the top of the main screen, beside the Rosetta logo (see Raspberry Pi Temperature ). A Raspberry Pi that gets too hot slows itself down (near 85 °C), and that can cause a Joint following error or a lost-contact E-stop part way through a program. Rosetta warns you before it gets that hot: Warning at (default 75 °C) - the temperature turns orange, and LinuxCNC shows a notice in the bottom right corner of its window. Alarm at (default 82 °C) - the temperature turns red, a message box appears, and, if Sound the alarm on the speaker is ticked, an alarm sounds on the Pi's speaker. Nothing is paused or stopped - a program carries on. Each warning appears once; it appears again only after the temperature has dropped a few degrees and risen again. Notes: The Warning temperature must be below the Alarm temperature. If it isn't, Warning must be below Alarm - not saved. is shown, and nothing is saved until it is corrected. These choices are saved as soon as you make them, and take effect the next time Rosetta starts. For what to do when the Pi gets hot, see The Pi is warm or too hot . Measurement System Select if you wish measurements to be performed using Imperial or metric values. This is normally set once, when the machine is first set up, so it is the last item in this section. Running the Rosetta Settings Program To access the Rosetta Settings program, there are 2 options: Option 1: Use the icon on the desktop Double-click on the Rosetta Settings icon on the desktop (the big gear) Option 2: Use the Applications menu Click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen Click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta Click on Rosetta Settings (the one with a gear) Applications Rosetta > Rosetta Rosetta Settings Backup Rosetta Update Rosetta Geometric Chuck Designer Rosette Designer"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/Overview.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Overview","h":[["Design-goals","Design goals"],["Rosetta-is-more-than-the-software-or-the-hardware","Rosetta is more than the software or the hardware"],["What-are-some-examples-of-how-it-can-be-used","What are some examples of how it can be used?"],["What-is-Rosetta-not-designed-to-do","What is Rosetta not designed to do?"],["Why-is-LinuxCNC-being-used","Why is LinuxCNC being used?"],["What-is-the-underlying-technology-being-used","What is the underlying technology being used?"],["Notes","Notes"]],"x":"Rosetta was developed by ornamental turners for use with a rose engine. It is designed to provide the ornamental turner a way for synchronizing activities on the rose engine using stepper motors, in a manner which also assists the ornamental turner with reducing the workload for onerous tasks. Design goals We designed Rosetta to provide the ornamental turner with the ability to: Execute relatively simple movements using menu options. Remove the mundane tasks from the ornamental turner’s activities, increasing the likelihood that the final product will have fewer errors. An example is adding a turk’s head design onto the end of a piece, and skipping every 3 rd index. Implement more complex designs using standard gCode (such as software simulation of rosettes or a geometric chuck). We designed Rosetta to be one which: Is powerful enough to Drive simultaneous movement on up to 10 axes ① , ② (AKA, channels), each of which can be set to any of: 2 spindles Traditional spindle (Sp0) Rosette phaser/multiplier (Sp1) Linear axes: X, Z, U, V, or W Rotary axes: A, B, or C Handle inputs from up to 6 limit switches Accommodate the use of an E-Stop button Use a signal tower with 2 lights and a siren to help the user monitor long-running activities (and alert the user when things go awry). Can control complex movement using standard gCode. Is integrated with the devices provided by ornamental turning vendors. Can have the software upgraded easily by the user as new functionality becomes available. Rosetta is more than the software or the hardware The ornamental turner needs a system which is not just powerful, but which has a set of tools surrounding it to make it functionally easy to use. To that end, there are additional pieces which have been provided. Designed with the ornamental turner in mind The system configuration files are developed around the needs of an ornamental turner using a rose engine. These functions are not typical for such control systems, but have been implemented on Rosetta. Online user manual There is an on-line user manual which will be continually updated. And a user forum There is an on-line user forum where users can share information. Based on Debian Linux, but easy to use Turk’s head Though it is based on Debian Linux, the user interface has been customized so that the usage of the system will be very intuitive (you only need to double click on the Turk's head icon on the desktop). If you know how to use Linux, that's OK, but it is not required to use the system. Note: The Rosetta emblem is a Turk’s head - a pattern cut with an eccentric cutting frame. (See also, ECF on Flat Surfaces in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge .) Supports very complex work using gCode Rosetta is an implementation of LinuxCNC, and thusly it allows for the use of standard gCode. The LinuxCNC gCode language is based on the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology’s RS274/NGC language, and is also outlined in the International Organization for Standardization’s standard, ISO 6983-1 . You do not need to study those; instead there is a gcode quick reference to help you if you choose to go this path. We are providing an online library for gCode programs developed by ornamental turners, allowing for re-use of the code by other ornamental turners. The intent is that the gCode will be documented in such a way that users can easily take a bit of gCode and modify it as needed for their own needs. This library will continue to grow over time. OT equipment vendor support We are working with vendors of ornamental turning hardware to have their parts designed for use with Rosetta. For example, a curvilinear slide could come with a stepper motor attached, and also have the necessary jack to plug it into Rosetta. We have also architected the configuration files so that updated files can be supplied by the vendors when they supply the hardware (e.g., a new definition for the Z axis could be supplied with the curvilinear slide, ensuring that the configuration works as expected). A system for now ... and the future Rosetta’s software can be upgraded using an easy mouse click from the Applications menu. And unlike many annoying systems we had at work, this system will not constantly harass you to upgrade; and the upgrades will not take many hours to complete. We selected vendors for all the components (especially the cabling) which show reliability for being around for a while, and supplying quality parts at reasonable prices. Buy it off-the-shelf We want Rosetta to be one which can be purchased as a completed system, needing no user design nor development. Just plug it in and go. And we are designing it so that you can expand as your needs grow. The Expansion Box ② can be added later if you find the Base Box ① does not support enough axes of movement. What are some examples of how it can be used? Some examples of the way Rosetta can be used include: Run the rose engine as a typical machine: Spindle by itself, Rosette phaser/multiplier (RPM) by itself, or Spindle and RPM together (which can be at different speeds). Index one of the axes of movement: Spindle by itself, RPM by itself (i.e., phasing the rosette relative to the object, as would be done when using a crossing wheel ), Spindle and RPM together, Any linear axis (X, Z, U, V, or W), or Any rotary / angular axis (A, B, or C). Coordinate the movement of any of the axes, or make other complex movements possible, through the use of gCode (e.g., a software simulation of a geometric chuck). Add threads to an object with many advanced options available but not required. Using the Rosette Designer , the ornamental turner can use emulate the use of an actual, physical rosette to confirm the design imparted meets their artistic goals. And then, the designed rosette can be exported for 3D printing or machining. What is Rosetta not designed to do? Rosetta is not intended to be used to replace the ornamental turner. It is not a system built to enable the user to develop a complete design, load it into Rosetta, and then press GO . We believe that workmanship at risk ③ makes for the most beautiful products. Certainly, a user could develop a sufficiently complex gCode program which would do all the work for the user, making it work similarly to what is done for 3D printing or a CNC woodworking router. But even that has risk in the ornamental turning world. Wood is a natural material, and the opportunity for a problem is ever present. An operator needs to be managing the process, not leaving it to a computer. But more importantly, the process of ornamental turning should be fun. Rosetta is designed to help you with that journey. Why is LinuxCNC being used? LinuxCNC ... Can be configured to meet the design goals needed for use on a rose engine, without having to develop a set of programs which may be difficult to support later. Is powerful enough to handle the design goals we established for Rosetta. Has been around for many years, having been initially designed by the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology in 1993. It has a strong user community with good support. And it continues to be upgraded to provide support needed for new computing hardware. Continues to support the same basic ideas and interfaces as it has for years, and it does not require the user to re-learn the system each time it is upgraded. Runs on a very stable and powerful operating system (which also does not require relearning to use when upgraded). Uses off-the-shelf, readily available parts. Does not have a high license cost to get started, nor a high maintenance cost for continued use. All this provides for a system which can meet the current needs at a reasonable cost, and provide for good long-term support. What is the underlying technology being used? Rosetta uses: LinuxCNC , an open-source system which runs on Debian Linux . Linux is an operating system which runs on general purpose computers like a PC or Raspberry Pi . A Raspberry Pi 5 , which is a powerful, low cost, general-purpose computer. The Raspberry Pi 5 has shown it is powerful enough for the computations needed to make LinuxCNC run smoothly. Readily available, off-the-shelf parts: Mesa 7i92TH Programmable I/O Card Stepper Online DM542T Stepper Motor Drivers Standard computer monitor Standard computer keyboard and mouse A custom printed circuit board (PCB), developed to make the wiring of the system easier, faster, and more reliable. Notes The Base Box supports 6 channels (axes) 3 limit switches 1 E-stop button a signal tower with two lights and a siren The Expansion Box adds capabilities for 4 channels (axes) 3 limit switches Workmanship at risk is an idea outlined by David Pye in his book, “The Nature and Art of Workmanship”."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/PowerOn.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Power On (F2)","h":[],"x":"Power on is used to “power the machine on”. This is always off when the system is started, and toggling the E-STOP does not affect this. You can either click on the indicated icon, or use the F2 key to toggle this. Whenever an error is encountered, the system moves to a powered off state also. It is a good practice to clear the error first (by clicking on the X for the error)."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/ReleaseNotes.html","m":"User Manual","t":"What's New in Rosetta","h":[["Which-version-do-I-have","Which version do I have?"],["O-S-1-0-Beta-1-12-17-November-2026","O/S 1.0 Beta 1.12 - 17 November 2026"],["O-S-1-0-date-TBD","O/S 1.0 - (date TBD)"]],"x":"This page lists what is new in each version of the Rosetta software, with the newest version at the top. When you update your system , you get every change listed here, up to the newest version. Which version do I have? Your version is shown at the top of the main screen, under Rosetta , and at the top of each page of the Rosetta Settings program. It looks like this: O/S 1.0 Beta 1.3 - 04 November 2026 Note: The date is when that version was released, so a version listed below with a later date is newer than yours. To get it, update your system . O/S 1.0 Beta 1.12 - 17 November 2026 What’s New Geometric Chuck Designer - a software geometric chuck. Set up to 5 stages, see the pattern they make, and save it as a G-code program that cuts it on the face with C and X. Presets give classic patterns to start from. Find it under Applications > Rosetta. Check Circle - a button in both the Geometric Chuck Designer and the Rosette Designer . It saves a small program that draws a circle round C's axis, so you can set the cutter at the right starting radius by measuring the circle (see Setting the Start Radius ). Shut Down - at the bottom of the Applications menu, and the red power symbol on the bar at the bottom of the screen. It shuts the computer down properly before you switch the power off (see Switching the System Off ). What’s Changed Rosette Designer, Ellipse : set by A (half its length) and B (half its width) under Ellipse Parameters. A second rocking rosette can ride on the ellipse, in its own column, with its lobes spaced evenly along the ellipse's outline and a Phase that slides the pattern along it. Rosette Designer, Phase : given as a percentage of a lobe (50% is half a lobe) instead of a fraction. Rosette Designer: \"Advance and Retract\" is now Advance and Retract Cutter . Backup Rosetta and Update Rosetta are no longer on the desktop - use the Applications menu (click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta). Rosetta and Rosetta Settings stay on the desktop. What’s Fixed Rosette Designer, rosette on an ellipse: the note under the drawings now gives the right radius to start the cutter when the pattern starts on a wave's deepest point (for example a D rosette at Phase 0%). Started at A, the waves came out past the ellipse. O/S 1.0 - (date TBD) The original version of the Rosetta software. Everything described in this manual is part of this version."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/RestoringKeyUserFilesFromAUSBFlashDrive.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Restoring Key User Files from a USB Flash Drive","h":[["Before-you-start","Before you start"],["Restoring-a-backup","Restoring a backup"],["After-the-restore-finishes-the-USB-flash-drive-is-ejected","After the restore finishes: the USB flash drive is ejected"],["What-the-restore-does-not-change","What the restore does not change"],["A-backup-from-a-different-machine","A backup from a different machine"]],"x":"The Restore from USB command copies a backup made by Back Up to USB from a USB flash drive back onto the Raspberry Pi. Use it, for example, after the Raspberry Pi's memory card has been replaced, or to move your own files onto a new Rosetta system. It puts back the same files Back Up to USB copies: your settings files, your gcode programs, your HAL additions and tool table, and your subroutines and user M-codes. See What Gets Copied for the full list. Before you start Do not run Restore from USB while LinuxCNC is running. LinuxCNC saves its settings as it closes, which would undo the restore. Close LinuxCNC first. (Rosetta Settings cannot be opened while LinuxCNC is running anyway, and Restore from USB checks again before it starts.) Do not remove the USB flash drive while the restore is running. Wait until Rosetta says it is safe to remove it. Note: Every file the restore replaces is first copied to the folder /home/reuben/Documents/Before_Restore, in a folder named with the date and time of the restore. If you restore the wrong backup, your previous files are still there. Restoring a backup Plug the USB flash drive that holds the backup into the Raspberry Pi. Open the Rosetta Settings program. Click on File at the top left corner of the Rosetta Settings window. Click on Restore from USB... The Restore from USB window opens. Under USB stick , pick the USB flash drive (if more than one is plugged in). Under Backup , pick the backup to restore. Each backup is listed by the date and time it was made, newest first. The window shows how many files of each kind the backup holds. Click Restore . A box lists what will be replaced. Click Restore again to go ahead, or Cancel to stop. Wait for the Restore complete box, then click OK . The USB flash drive is ejected for you, so it can be removed (see After the restore finishes below). Rosetta Settings closes by itself. Open it again to see the restored settings. Note: Rosetta Settings closes after a restore so that it cannot save the settings it was showing over the ones just restored. If you have changed any Scale, speed, acceleration or PID values without saving them, Restore from USB asks first whether to save them. After the restore finishes: the USB flash drive is ejected When the restore finishes, Rosetta ejects the USB flash drive. Ejecting means the Raspberry Pi has finished with the flash drive and has disconnected from it, so that it is safe to pull out. The flash drive is still plugged in, but the Raspberry Pi can no longer use it: it no longer shows in the File Manager, or in the Back Up to USB or Restore from USB windows. Do not pull the USB flash drive out while the restore is running. Wait until Rosetta says It is now safe to remove the USB stick. To use the USB flash drive again - for example, to make another backup, to restore a backup, or to copy a gcode program from it - you have to take it out and put it back in: Pull the USB flash drive out of the Raspberry Pi. Wait about 5 seconds. Plug the USB flash drive back into the Raspberry Pi. Wait a few seconds for the Raspberry Pi to find it. A File Manager window may open showing what is on the flash drive; you can close that window. The USB flash drive can now be used again. Note: If Rosetta says The stick could not be ejected automatically , eject it yourself before pulling it out: open the File Manager, and in the list on the left side of its window, click the eject symbol (⏏) next to the USB flash drive's name. When the flash drive's name disappears from the list, it is safe to pull it out. What the restore does not change Files on the Raspberry Pi that are not in the backup are left as they are. For example, a gcode program you saved after the backup was made is kept. Backups made before Rose Engine Butler was renamed Rosetta (in a folder named REB_Backup on the USB flash drive) can be restored too. Their old file names are changed to the new ones as they are copied. A backup from a different machine If the backup's axis selection (which axis uses each plug) or expansion box setting is different from this machine's, the Restore from USB window says so in red, listing each difference - for example, Channel 04: C here, B in the backup. Do not restore a backup from a machine set up differently unless you mean to. The restore copies that machine's axis selection along with its other settings. After restoring, check the Axis Selection tab in Rosetta Settings, and change it to match how this machine's motors are plugged in, before you start LinuxCNC."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/RosettaMainScreen.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Main Screen","h":[["Axes-Colors","Axes Colors"],["At-Startup","At Startup"],["After-Startup","After Startup"],["Raspberry-Pi-Temperature","Raspberry Pi Temperature"],["ENA_","ENA"],["Feed-Rate","Feed Rate"],["Run-Operation","Run Operation"],["Device","Device"]],"x":"Axes Colors Color Axis Movement Control Neg Pos Neg Pos Yellow X U A Red V B Blue Z W C Black Spindles (Sp0 & Sp1) On this page, the axis letters are colored to assist the user. (Note: LinuxCNC does not allow for a Y axis when running as a lathe.) Every axis has a row of controls on the user screens, but only the axes selected for use can be operated - the others are grayed out. For example, in the screen shown below, the user has selected to use the X, Z, U, V, W, and C axes, so the A and B rows are grayed out. The table at the bottom of the user screen titled, Axis Assignments , shows the current axis assignments. It is updated the next time Rosetta is started after the selected axes are changed. At Startup When the Rosetta system is started, the screen shown below will be displayed. At this point, the LinuxCNC system is still in an \"Estop\" and \"off\" state. System at startup (Base Box) After releasing the Estop (F1) , and powering the system on (F2) , the same screen will no longer be grayed out, and will be as shown below. Note : This state can be also encountered if an error is encountered. After Startup After releasing the Estop (F1) , and powering the system on (F2) , the screen below will be shown. System after startup (Base Box with Expansion Box) For the panel on the right side of the screen, all the axes which Rosetta supports are shown. The axes which are not assigned to a channel using the Rosetta Settings are grayed out. The controls on this panel to the right are outlined below. Raspberry Pi Temperature There is a Raspberry Pi inside the Rosetta system. This is a small computer inside the Base Box that is used to run Rosetta. At the top right of the panel, beside the Rosetta logo, is the temperature of the Raspberry Pi (for example, Raspberry Pi 66 °C ). This displayed text changes colors if the Raspberry Pi’s temperature rises too high. Orange - the Raspberry Pi is warm (at or above the Warning temperature). Rosetta also shows a notice in the bottom right corner of its window. Red - the Raspberry Pi is too hot (at or above the Alarm temperature). A message box appears and an alarm sounds on the speaker. There is also an indicator if the Raspberry Pi’s supply voltage is not where either should be. ⚡ - the power supply isn't keeping up (undervoltage). There will also be a notice. Nothing is stopped by any of these. The temperature ranges used for alerting the user, and whether the alarm sounds, are set on the Other page of Rosetta Settings. Should any of these conditions occur, see The Pi is warm or too hot . ENA These buttons are used to enable or disable an axis. When red, the axis is disabled. When green, the axis is enabled. When gray, the axis is not configured for use. If you click an index, Sync Move or Cut Thread button which would move using an axis or spindle which is not enabled, one of two things will happen Axis Not Enabled Setting What Happens Ask a pop-up box names the axes which are not enabled, and offers two buttons: Enable ... and Run or Enable ... and Move . If this is selected, it turns those ENA buttons on, the same as clicking them, then carries on. Cancel . If this is selected, the ENAble buttons stay off, and nothing moves. Enable it automatically The ENA buttons are turned on with no box, and Rosetta carries on. For a Gcode program, a short note in the bottom right corner of the LinuxCNC window says which axes were enabled. Axis Not Enabled is set on Settings - Other . Feed Rate Used to set the feed rate (speed) for movements on this page. The unit of measure (UOM) for that speed is shown on the screen. These feed rates are not used on the Manual Control / Jogging (F3) nor the MDI Tab (F5) . The UOM shown, and used by the system, is based on the Measurement System selection. Run Operation These 3 buttons are used for running the spindles. Starts the spindle(s) running in the negative direction. It will continue running in this direction until stopped. Stops the spindle(s) from running. Starts the spindle(s) running in the positive direction. It will continue running in this direction until stopped. Notes for the Sp1 spindle (rosette phaser/multiplier): The feed rate for the Sp1 spindle is a percentage of the Sp0 spindle's speed. At 100%, they run the same speed. This feed rate can be set anywhere in the range of +1,000% to -1,000%. If the feed rate is set to 0, the Sp1 will not run. If the Sp1 spindle is disabled, it will not run. Device This allows the user to denote the use of the non-spindle axes. The values shown are in the Device Names list on Settings - Other ."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/RosetteDesigner.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Rosette Designer","h":[["Opening-the-Rosette-Designer","Opening the Rosette Designer"],["Designing-a-Rosette","Designing a Rosette"],["Rosette_","Rosette"],["The-Ellipse","The Ellipse"],["Movement_","Movement"],["Depth_","Depth"],["Advance-and-Retract-Cutter","Advance and Retract Cutter"],["Object-Size","Object Size"],["Notes-Under-the-Drawings","Notes Under the Drawings"],["Set-the-Backlash-First","Set the Backlash First"],["Setting-the-Start-Radius-Check-Circle","Setting the Start Radius: Check Circle"],["Saving-and-Running-the-Program","Saving and Running the Program"],["Saving-the-Rosette-for-3D-Printing","Saving the Rosette for 3D Printing"],["The-Settings","The Settings"],["Saving_","Saving"],["Things-to-Know","Things to Know"],["Changing-a-Saved-Rosette","Changing a Saved Rosette"]],"x":"On a rose engine, a rosette is a cam: as the work turns, a rubber follows the rosette's edge or face, and the machine rocks or pumps to follow it (see the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge's Rosettes page). The Rosette Designer makes a software rosette instead: you choose its shape, and it saves a G-code program in which C turns the work while the cutter follows the rosette: Rocking - the cutter follows the rosette in X. Pumping - the cutter follows the rosette in Z. Both - X follows one rosette and Z another, as with two rosettes engaged at once. Use it to try a rosette before buying or making one, or to use a rosette you would need too rarely to be worth making. Opening the Rosette Designer Click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen. Click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta. Click on Rosette Designer . The Rosette Designer only writes G-code files, so it can be open while Rosetta is running. Only one Rosette Designer window can be open. If one is already open, for example behind Rosetta, opening it again brings that window to the front, with a message saying it was already open. Designing a Rosette The Rosette Designer fills the screen, like Rosetta Settings. The drawings in the middle change as you change the settings on the left, and the notes on the right explain each section, with a button that opens this page at that section. The drawings: The rosette shows how far the cutter moves as the work turns once. The cut shows what the cut will look like on the work. Rosette With Motion set to Both (see Movement, below), there are two columns of these settings: the Rocking rosette (X) and the Pumping rosette (Z) , each with its own shape, lobes, amplitude and invert, and the pumping rosette also has a phase. The drawing shows both rosettes - X in blue, Z in red. With an Ellipse there is one more column - see The Ellipse , below. Shape The shape of the rosette. The Holtzapffel letters are the patterns in Holtzapffel & Co.'s guide to their rose cutting frame: A - a smooth wave (a sine). C - sharp points with hollow sides. D - round petals meeting in sharp cusps. F - groups of three waves, the middle one notched, like a Cupid's bow. F with 1 lobe (F-1) is a single heart. J - waves on arcs. K - a waved polygon. M and N - scallops on lobes (M's lobes are longer). O and P - groups of swelling scallops or waves. Q - scallops on arcs. S - a scalloped heart. For more on each letter, and what its number counts, see Holtzapffel Rosette Letters in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge. The other shapes: Plain polygon - straight sides between points. Lotus - straight sides from point to point, with a small V notch in the middle of each lobe. Tudor - a rounded petal for each lobe, with a small sharp barb between the petals, like the sepals of a Tudor rose. Ellipse - a true ellipse (see The Ellipse , below). Lotus, Tudor and F-1 cut the pattern that the MDF Rose Engine's rosette of that name draws on a paper chuck (see the MDF Rose Engine rosettes ). Lobes How many lobes around the work, counted the way the Holtzapffel guide numbers its rosettes - the small waves around the edge. For example, J24 has 24 waves, on 4 arcs of 6. Shapes made of groups (J, M, N, O, P and Q) only take whole groups, so Lobes steps by a group. Amplitude How far the cutter moves, from the rosette's highest point to its lowest - the amount the cut moves at the work, not at the rosette. Phase There is no phase setting for a single rosette. To put the pattern where you want it on the work, index the work on the Rosetta panel before running the program - this does what the phasing holes on a physical rosette do. With Motion set to Both , the pumping rosette has a Phase : it turns the pumping rosette relative to the rocking rosette, either as % of a lobe (50 is half a lobe) or in degrees of the spindle. For example, half a lobe of a 7-lobed rosette is 50% of a lobe, or 25.71 degrees. Indexing can't do this, since it turns both rosettes together. For shapes made of groups (J, M, N, O, P, Q and S), the phase can go past 100%, moving through the group - up to 600% for J's groups of 6. The rosette on an Ellipse also has a Phase - see The Ellipse , below. Invert Turns points into hollows. This is the same as running the rosette with the cutter on the opposite side of the spindle's axis of rotation - far side cutting instead of near side. Straight sides For a plain polygon rocking on the face, sets the amplitude that makes its sides straight at the Radius (see Object Size, below). Rubber The rubber that follows the rosette. For now, the only choice is a Point , which follows the shape exactly. The Ellipse Choose Ellipse as the Shape of the rocking rosette. An Ellipse only rocks (X): with Motion set to Pumping , it doesn't move the cutter. Under Ellipse Parameters : A - half the ellipse's length: from its centre to the end of its length. B - half its width: from its centre to its side. For example, an ellipse 4\" long and 2\" wide has A = 2 and B = 1. The note under the drawings gives its length, width and the distance between its foci. The rosette on the ellipse. With an Ellipse, a second column appears beside it: the Rocking rosette on the ellipse (X) . Its waves are cut in from the ellipse, so the ellipse is the outside of the pattern. Shape - any shape, or None for the plain ellipse. Lobes - spaced evenly along the ellipse's outline, so on a long ellipse they don't crowd together along its sides. Amplitude - how far the waves cut in from the ellipse. Phase - slides the pattern along the outline, as % of a lobe or in degrees (360 is the whole outline). For example, at 0% a D rosette has a cusp at each end of the ellipse; at 50% it has a petal there. Invert - turns points into hollows, as above. With Motion set to Both , the Pumping rosette (Z) is a third column. Its lobes are spaced along the outline too, so the two stay in step. D12 on A = 1, B = 0.5, Phase 0% The same, Phase 50% Where to start the cutter. The note under the drawings says where to put the cutter: radius A from the centre, or less when the pattern starts on a wave's deepest point - for example, a D rosette at Phase 0% starts on a cusp, so the cutter starts in by the whole amplitude. Put the cutter on the line you want the ellipse's length to lie along, and use the check circle to set the radius. Do not expect an exact A by B ellipse unless the cutter starts at the radius the note gives. The program moves the cutter from wherever it is put, by the same amounts at any radius - as a physical ellipse rosette does - so the length always comes out 2 x (A - B) more than the width. Started further out, the cut is rounder; further in, it is longer and narrower, and then a peanut and a figure 8. Note: with A = 1 and B = 0.5, the length is always 1\" more than the width: started at the right radius the cut is 2\" x 1\"; started further out, perhaps 2 3/8\" x 1 3/8\". Movement Motion Rocking (X) , Pumping (Z) , or Both - a rocking rosette and a pumping rosette at the same time. Cutting on Face (the end of the work) or Side . This sets which axis goes deeper into the work - Z on the face, X on the side - and how the cut is drawn. C speed How fast C turns the work while cutting, in revolutions per minute. C direction Which way C turns: + or - . Depth Choose one: Passes, deeper each time The program cuts the rosette several times, going Depth per pass deeper before each pass, at the Plunge feed . The first pass is already one step deep, so put the cutter just touching the work. At the end, the cutter backs out to where it started. Finishing pass : tick it to run the last pass slower, for a better finish - at a percentage of C speed (10% to start). The earlier passes run at full C speed. Spring passes : 0 to 5 extra turns at the final depth, to clean up the cut. They run at the finishing speed when Finishing pass is ticked. Turns at one depth The program cuts the rosette the chosen number of times without going deeper - as with a physical rosette. To go deeper, move the cutter in yourself and run the program again. Deeper is + : normally the cutter goes deeper by moving the depth axis in its minus direction. Tick this if going deeper means moving it in its plus direction. Advance and Retract Cutter To start the cutter clear of the work, set how far it moves in before cutting - X advance , Z advance or both - and which way: Towards centre or Away for X, Towards headstock or Away for Z. Leave both at 0 for no advance. Put the cutter that far clear of where the pattern is to be cut. Run the program. The cutter moves in at the Plunge feed - the depth axis last (Z on the face, X on the side), so it enters the work along the depth direction. The passes are cut from there - their depth is measured from where the advance ends. At the end, the cutter moves back out to exactly where you put it, the depth axis first. Do not run the program until you have checked each advance's direction. The cutter moves the whole advance distance that way before it starts cutting - set the wrong way, it moves into the work instead of up to it. Note: If the program is stopped part way, the cutter is left where it stopped - it moves back out only at the normal end. Object Size Radius is the radius of the object being made - the piece you are cutting - and Radius is the says which part of the cut it is: Outside (largest) - where the cut comes furthest out from the centre. Inside (smallest) - where the cut comes closest in. If you are making a rosette to use later on a rose engine, the object being made is that rosette: enter the radius of the rosette you want to make. Rose engines differ - some measure their rosettes across the points (use Outside), others across the hollows (use Inside). A physical rosette usually has phasing holes, which let it be turned relative to the work on the rose engine. The G-code program doesn't cut them - add them as you would for any rosette, or use Save for 3D Printing , which puts them in. (Patterns cut with Rosetta itself don't need them: index on the Rosetta panel instead.) Radius is only used for the drawing, for Straight sides and for the note on where to start the cutter - the program cuts wherever the cutter is. When cutting on the face with a rocking rosette, the note under the drawings says where to put the cutter so that the outside or inside of the cut is at that radius. Note: with an Ellipse cut on the face, A and B set the size, so Object Size is greyed out. Note: The same rosette cuts a different shape at a different radius - sharper and more hollow closer to the centre, rounder further out. This is the de la Condamine effect (see The de la Condamine Effect in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge). So choose the Amplitude for the Radius you will cut at, and check the drawing - it shows the shape at that radius. Notes Under the Drawings Under the drawings, the Rosette Designer says how long one turn and the whole program will take (counting a slower finishing pass), where to start the cutter, and warns you when: C, or the axis the rosette or the depth needs, isn't assigned to a channel in Axis Selection - the program won't run until it is. C's speed is above its Max Speed, or the rosette is too steep for X or Z to follow at that speed. The program still runs, but C slows down where needed, so it takes longer. Set the Backlash First A rosette program reverses the rocking or pumping axis twice for every lobe - a 12-lobe rosette reverses it 24 times each turn. Each time it reverses, any slack in the slide (play in the leadscrew, nut or gears) is taken up before the cutter moves again, so every lobe is cut slightly flat on one side and the curves come out uneven. Rosetta can make up for that slack. Before cutting a rosette, set the Backlash for the axis that follows it - X for rocking, Z for pumping, both for Both: Measure the axis's lost motion: with a dial indicator on the slide, jog one way, then reverse and note how far you jog before the indicator moves. Open Rosetta Settings (Rosetta must be closed) and go to the Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning page. Enter the measurement in that axis's Backlash box. It is saved at once and used the next time Rosetta starts. These two Holtzapffel A-12 drawings were cut on a paper chuck by the same program - the first with X's Backlash at 0, the second with it set. The waves of the second are even and smooth: No Backlash Backlash set Note: Backlash matters most for small, fine patterns, where the slack is a large part of each wave. It doesn't need to be exact - a little less than the measured lost motion is better than too much, which makes the slide jump at each reversal. For more on backlash, and what compensation can't fix, see Backlash in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge. Setting the Start Radius: Check Circle For a rocking rosette on the face, the program moves the cutter from wherever it is put, so the cutter's distance from C's axis - the start radius the note under the drawings gives - sets the pattern's size and shape. That distance is hard to measure, especially when the headstock moves. The Check Circle... button makes it easy: it saves a small program that turns C once with X and Z standing still, so the cutter draws a circle round C's axis. The circle's diameter is twice the cutter's radius. Press Check Circle... . It saves a program named for the size the circle should be, for example Check-Circle_3.000in.ngc , in the gcode folder, and offers to open it in Rosetta. Put the cutter just touching the work, where the cut will start. Run the check circle program, and measure the circle's diameter. If the circle is smaller than it should be, move X out by half the difference; if it is bigger, move X in. For example, if a circle that should be 3\" across comes out 2 1/8\", move X out by 7/16\". Run the check circle again, until it is the right size. Open the rosette program and run it, without moving X. Note: with an X advance , set the circle first, then move X by the advance, as the check circle's message says, before running the rosette. Note: the check circle cuts a light circle in the work. On a paper chuck that doesn't matter; on finished work, set the radius on a scrap piece first. Note: the check circle works on any machine - see Working from Where the Cutter Starts in the Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge. Saving and Running the Program Press Save G-code... . The Rosette Designer suggests a name, such as Rosette-Lotus8_rock_face.ngc or Rosette-HoltzD12-Ellipse50_rock_face.ngc , in the gcode folder. If Rosetta is running, the Rosette Designer offers to open the program in Rosetta. Otherwise, open it in Rosetta with File > Open . Put the cutter where the pattern should be cut - at the radius the note under the drawings gives (use the check circle ). Do not start the program until the cutter is where you want the pattern. The program works from wherever the cutter is when it starts - the same as setting up a cut with a physical rosette. Run the program. Note: while the program runs, the progress bar on the Rosetta panel shows which pass it is on, for example Program 42% - pass 2 of 3 , then finishing pass 3 of 3 . Do not name the program starting with \"Rosetta\", and do not name it M100 through M199. Those names are reserved (see Running Gcode Programs ), and the Rosette Designer refuses them. Note: C is not turned back at the end: after three turns, C reads 1080 degrees. The work is in the same place, and the next program again works from wherever C is. Saving the Rosette for 3D Printing Save for 3D Printing... saves the rosette itself - not a program that cuts its pattern - so you can 3D print it, or have it made, and use it on a rose engine. It saves two kinds of file: STL - for a 3D printer. Open it in your printer's slicer. STEP - for CAD programs, and for a machine shop that makes rosettes from metal or wood. Both files are in millimetres , whatever units the design uses - slicers expect STL files in millimetres. The kind of rosette depends on the design's Motion : Rocking - a disc whose edge follows the pattern. Pumping - a disc with a raised rim whose top rises and falls with the pattern. The rim stands at least 1 mm above the rest of the disc. Both - each rosette is saved on its own; choose Rocking (X) or Pumping (Z) at the top of the window. The Settings Machine - fills in your rose engine's usual rosette. MDF Rose Engine fills in everything - its size, thickness, bore, phasing holes and mounting slots, as on its rosette drawing. The Armbruster Mark II and the Lindow fill in only their size. Choose Other to enter your own; changing anything the machine filled in changes it to Other. Diameter and Measured at - the rosette's size, across the points ( Largest ) or across the hollows ( Smallest ). Rose engines differ: measure one of your own rosettes the way your machine's maker does. A pumping rosette's diameter is always its outside. Machine ratio - the rosette's wave is the design's Amplitude times this. On most rose engines the rosette moves more or less than the cutter does, set by the machine's levers. To find yours, cut with a rosette whose wave you know and measure the cut. 1 makes the rosette's wave the same as the cut's. Thickness - how thick the disc is. Centre bore - the diameter of the hole in the middle, to fit your rose engine's rosette arbor. Rim width (pumping only) - how wide the patterned rim is, in from the edge. Phasing holes : Spacing - A quarter lobe apart (the MDF Rose Engine's way): each hole turns the pattern a quarter lobe on from the last - 0, 1/4, 1/2, 3/4, 1 lobe, and on. So the spacing depends on the rosette's lobes: 11.25 degrees on an 8-lobe rosette, 10 on a 9-lobe one, 22.5 on a 4-lobe one. Each hole is a whole number of lobes plus a quarter, a half or three quarters from where the pattern starts (marked Start on the drawing), so the hole at the start phases it by 0. On a rosette with many lobes, holes a quarter lobe apart would touch, so they go 1 1/4 lobes apart instead (or 2 1/4...) - each still turns the pattern a quarter lobe on, and the window says so. Evenly round spaces them equally instead. Number - how many; 0 means none. Nine a quarter lobe apart phase the pattern from 0 to 2 lobes. On circle - the diameter of the circle their centres are on. Hole diameter - each hole's true size (the MDF Rose Engine uses a #30 drill, 0.1285\"). Centred at - where round the rosette the holes are grouped, in degrees: 0 is where the pattern starts (to the right on the drawing), 90 the top. The MDF Rose Engine's are at 0, midway between its two slots. Mounting slots - arced slots for the screws that hold the rosette to its drive gear: how many (evenly round from the top; 0 means none), the diameter of the circle along their middles, their Span in degrees (from the centre of one rounded end to the other), and their width (0.266\" lets a 1/4-20 screw through). The MDF Rose Engine has two, top and bottom, spanning 90 degrees on a 5\" circle. Printed hole allowance - printed holes come out smaller than drawn. This much is added to the bore, the phasing holes and the slots' width in the STL file, so they print the right size. Start with 0.008\" (0.2 mm), print one, and adjust it to suit your printer. The STEP file keeps their true sizes, for machining. Save as - tick STL, STEP or both. The drawing shows the rosette seen from the top, as it will be printed. On a pumping rosette, darker parts of the rim are higher. Under the settings, the window gives the rosette's size and how deep its wave is, and how much larger the STL's holes are. It shows problems in red, and Save... stays greyed out until they are fixed - for example, phasing holes that run into the bore or the edge, or that overlap. Warnings in orange don't stop you saving. Saving Set the design as usual, then press Save for 3D Printing... . Set the rosette's size, mounting and phasing holes, and check the drawing. Press Save... . The Rosette Designer suggests a name, such as Rosette-HoltzD12_rock , in the Rosettes folder in Documents. It adds .stl and .step . Copy the files to the computer you print or design with. Back Up to USB also copies the Rosettes folder. The settings are kept with the design, so the window opens with them next time, and they are saved in the G-code program too. Things to Know The edge is shaped for a point rubber. A real rubber's rounded nose can't reach right into narrow hollows, so the printed rosette cuts a little softer than the drawing. Which way round. Seen from the top as printed, the rosette matches the drawing in the Rosette Designer. For a shape that isn't the same both ways round - the F-1 heart, or S - try it on a paper chuck first to see which face goes towards you. Fine waves. Printers hold about 0.1 mm (0.004\"), so a wave much smaller than that may not print. The window warns you. Wear. Plastic wears against the rubber. A printed rosette suits trying a design before making it in metal or wood. Do not print a pumping rosette at a coarse layer height. A pumping rosette is printed lying flat, so its patterned rim is built up in layers that leave ridges as it rises and falls. Print it at the smallest layer height your printer can, and use a rubber that rides over what ridges remain - for example, one with a roller bearing on its nose. Changing a Saved Rosette Press Open... and choose a program the Rosette Designer saved. Its settings come back, ready to change and save again. The Rosette Designer also remembers every setting of the last rosette you worked on, so it opens the way you left it. Press New to start again from the standard rosette. Note: only programs saved by the Rosette Designer can be opened this way."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/RunningGcodePrograms.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Running Gcode Programs","h":[["Storing-a-Gcode-Program-on-Rosetta","Storing a Gcode Program on Rosetta"],["Naming-Your-Gcode-Programs","Naming Your Gcode Programs"],["Editing-a-Gcode-Program","Editing a Gcode Program"],["Running-a-Gcode-Program","Running a Gcode Program"],["If-an-axis-is-not-available-for-use","If an axis is not available for use"],["If-an-axis-is-not-enabled","If an axis is not enabled"],["Using-AI-to-Create-a-Gcode-Program","Using AI to Create a Gcode Program"],["Notes","Notes"]],"x":"With LinuxCNC, you can download a Gcode program from the Rosetta Gcode library ( rosetta.colvintools.com/Gcode ) and run that, or run a Gcode program you created. Storing a Gcode Program on Rosetta It is recommended that you store the programs in the default directory already setup: /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode/ Key Notes: Storing gcode files in this directory (or a sub-directory of this directory) will ensure the backup program will copy them to a USB flash drive. Gcode programs in other directories are not backed up using the backup program . The Gcode files for LinuxCNC have a file type of .ngc . Naming Your Gcode Programs A few file names are reserved for the Rosetta system's own use. LinuxCNC looks in the gcode directory first when it needs one of its own files, so a program of yours with one of these names could be used in place of the system's file, and features such as the Threading tab would not work correctly. When you name a Gcode program, do not start the file's name with Rosetta or rosetta (in upper or lower case, or any mix of the two). For example, Rosetta_Flower.ngc and rosetta_test.ngc should be renamed, to be something like Flower.ngc and test.ngc . do not name it M100 through M199 . LinuxCNC treats files with these names as custom M-codes. Any other name is fine. If you download a program whose name breaks one of these rules, rename it when you save the file to the gcode directory . Rosetta checks for these reserved names every time LinuxCNC starts. If one is found, what happens depends on the Startup File Check choice on the Other tab of Rosetta Settings: Abort startup (the default) - a message names the file(s) which are using reserved names, and LinuxCNC does not start until they are renamed. Warn, but let me continue - the same message is shown, but LinuxCNC starts anyway once you close it. A short sound plays with the message if startup is aborted - see Sounds . Key Note: Updating the Rosetta system never changes or removes the files in the gcode directory. Editing a Gcode Program Thunar Thunar You can list all the programs in the directory noted above using the file manager, Thunar. The easiest way to start using Thunar is hover your mouse at the bottom middle of the screen. When the bar pops up, click on the icon which looks like a file cabinet. Once Thunar is started, you can use the icons on the left side of the screen for Places , navigate to where the Gcode programs are stored. Click on Home then navigate down to /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode/ Alternatively, you can enter the directory in the location field (at the top of the screen) directly by entering the location noted above. Geany Then, on the one you wish to edit, right-click on it with your mouse, and choose the editor you wish to use. One editor included with the system is Geany , but you could install a different one if you prefer. Running a Gcode Program To run a Gcode program, LinuxCNC must be in a started mode ② . Press the O button ① on the keyboard to open the program, or Click on F ile on the top menu bar, then Click on O pen The Open window starts in the directory where Gcode programs are stored: /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode/ Click on the Gcode program you wish to run, and click the O pen button. After opening the file, the image on the Preview tab should be updated to show a simulation of the movement path. This is an approximation, but may be useful for you. If you open a program from a different directory, the Open window will start there for the rest of the session. It starts in the gcode directory again each time LinuxCNC is started. You may notice an error. For example, if the Gcode program is expecting to use the B axis but you do not have your system configured to use the B axis, this will generate an error. You will have to correct any errors for LinuxCNC to load the Gcode program. Gcode Go Button Press the R button ① on the keyboard to run the program, or click on the Go button. This is the one shown in the image to the right. If you prefer to step the Gcode program one step at a time, either press the T button ① , or use the button to the right of the Go button. If you experience errors with what is happening, this may be useful for you. Key Note: If you open a program from a different directory, the Open window will open that directory for the rest of the session. It will reset to defaulting in the gcode directory again each time LinuxCNC is started. If an axis is not available for use When you click O pen to open the gcode program you wish to run, Rosetta will check to confirm that the needed axes are configured for use. If an axis has not been assigned to one of the available channels, the gcode program cannot run. For example, If your Rosetta system is setup as: Channel 00 - X axis Channel 01 - Z axis Channel 02 - A axis Channel 03 - U axis Channel 04 - Sp0 Channel 05 - Sp1 And the gcode program you wish to run needs X, Z, and C to run, The program cannot be executed as there is no C axis. To remediate this, Exit Rosetta, Start the Rosetta Settings program. The Axis Selection tab will enable you to re-configure the axes so that X, Z, and C are available. Save the changes and Exit the Rosetta Settings Program Re-start Rosetta If an axis is not enabled When you click Go (or press R ), Rosetta checks which axes the program moves. If any of them has its ENA button off (red), a box such as C not enabled appears before anything moves: Click Enable C and Run (or press Enter) to turn C's ENA on and run the program. Click Cancel to leave it off. The program does not run. If Axis Not Enabled is set to Enable it automatically (on the Other tab of Rosetta Settings), there is no box: the ENA is turned on, a note in the bottom right corner of the LinuxCNC window says so, and the program runs. Note: The check uses the program's Preview . A rapid (G0) move made before the program's first cutting move is not shown in the Preview, so an axis moved only by such a move is not checked. Using AI to Create a Gcode Program The Claude code program was installed on the Rosetta system. To use it, on the Raspberry Pi, you will need a Claude coding license which is typically included in the Pro plan, but not the free plan. The free plan level can be used on a web browser to do similar work. Other AI programs may also work; however, we have not tested those. Notes Using either upper case or lower case letters for this is acceptable. If an error is encountered, LinuxCNC will usually power off. You will have to power on (F2) again to resume operations."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/SavingAndSharingSettings.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Saving and Sharing Settings","h":[["What-is-saved-when","What is saved when"],["The-buttons","The buttons"],["The-File-menu","The File menu"]],"x":"Key Note: The Settings program cannot be run when the Rosetta program is running. Some changes made in the Rosetta Settings program are saved as soon as you make them, and others only when you save them. The buttons for saving and loading settings are at the bottom of the Axis Selection tab, and are also in the File menu at the top left corner of the window. What is saved when Setting When it is saved Axis selection (which axis uses each plug), limit jacks, E-stop button, signal tower Immediately, as soon as you change them Backlash, jog speeds, measurement system, device names Immediately, as soon as you change them Scale, Max Vel, Max Accel, and PID values When you click Save All Settings . If you close the Settings program with unsaved changes, it asks whether to Save and Close , Close Without Saving , or Cancel . All settings are kept in the file /home/reuben/Documents/RosettaSet_v1.ini, which Rosetta reads each time it starts. The buttons Saves the Scale, Backlash, Max Vel, Max Accel, and PID values for every axis. Click this before closing the Settings program. Saves all your settings, then also saves a copy to a file you choose - for example, a dated backup before you re-tune the machine. Saves just the axes you pick (each with its Scale, Backlash, and tuning values), and optionally the Measurement System, into a small file. You can label it with one of your Device Names - useful for sharing the calibration for one device, such as a curvilinear slide, with another Rosetta user. Loads settings from a file saved by either of the \"Save ... As\" buttons, and applies them. Click Save All Settings afterwards to keep them. The File menu The File menu at the top left corner of the Rosetta Settings window has the same four commands as the buttons above, plus: Command What it does Back Up to USB... Copies your settings files, gcode programs, HAL additions, tool table, and subroutines onto a USB flash drive. See Backing Up Key User Files to a USB Flash Drive . Restore from USB... Copies a backup from a USB flash drive back onto the Raspberry Pi, then closes Rosetta Settings. See Restoring Key User Files from a USB Flash Drive . Quit Closes Rosetta Settings. If you have unsaved changes, it asks first. Note: The keyboard shortcuts shown in the menu also work: Ctrl+S (Save All Settings), Shift+Ctrl+S (Save All Settings As), Ctrl+O (Load Settings from File), and Ctrl+Q (Quit). Tip : once you have calibrated a device (see Scale Settings for a Device ), use Save Subset of Settings As... to keep its values in a file named after the device. Then, whenever you put that device back on the machine, load its file."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/ScaleAndMicrosteps.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Microstep Calculator","h":[["Stepper-Motor-Drive-Options","Stepper Motor Drive Options"],["Determining-the-Setting-to-Use","Determining the Setting to Use"],["Background","Background"],["Stepper-Motors","Stepper Motors"],["Stepper-Motor-Accuracy","Stepper Motor Accuracy"],["Spindle-Movement","Spindle Movement"],["Linear-Movement","Linear Movement"]],"x":"This program is used to determine the setting of the microsteps on the DM542T Stepper Driver, based on the device being used on your rose engine. It should be used to calculate the needed setting to achieve a granularity of movement lower than 0.0005”. Stepper Motor Drive Options Each DM542T Stepper Drives can be set to one of these settings. DM542T Microstepping Options Micro-steps Pulses / Motor Rev Micro-steps Pulses / Motor Rev Micro-steps Pulses / Motor Rev Micro-steps Pulses / Motor Rev 1 200 16 3,200 5 1,000 40 8,000 2 400 32 6,400 10 2,000 50 10,000 4 800 64 12,800 20 4,000 100 20,000 8 1,600 128 25,600 25 5,000 125 25,000 Input fields are colored . Once the values have been entered, click the Calculate Granularity button below the table. Fixed Values These are based on your machine DM542T Microsteps Setting 1 2 4 8 16 32 64 128 5 10 20 25 40 50 100 125 Stepper Motor Gearing Ratio :1 The gearing ratio for the stepper motor driving the moved part. For spindles on the MDF Rose Engine, this is 9:1, so a 9 would be entered here. Leadscrew Movement per Revolution inch This is the distance the cutter is moved for each revolution of the leadscrew. Leadscrew Starts Object Radius inch For cutting on the perimeter, this is the angular movement. Calculated Values Linear Movement per Microstep inch Angular Movement per Microstep inch Calculate Granularity Determining the Setting to Use The microsteps setting to use should be based on weighing accuracy vs. speed. Higher Number of Micro-steps Lower Number of Micro-steps A higher setting for the microsteps allows for a finer granularity of movement. There is a limit to the number of pulses which LinuxCNC can send to a stepper motor in a given timeframe (i.e., there is a maximum pulses/second). Defining the pulses per revolution lower allows LinuxCNC to drive the motion faster. Background Stepper Motors This calculator is based on stepper motors which have a step angle of 1.8°. The movement on those stepper motors is defined by this formula: Stepper Motor Accuracy If the DM542T is set to 8 microsteps, Spindle Movement If the spindle is driven by a belt giving a gearing ratio of 9:1, the movement per microstep is given by: Arc length can then be calculated for the perimeter of the piece which has a 2” radius as: Linear Movement For a linear axis movement, the movement is driven by a leadscrew which has these parameters: a distance of movement per revolution (in this case, 0.100” is used), and a number of starts for the threads (in this case, 4 is used). And this too can have a gearing ratio. In this case, a 6:1 ratio is used."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/ScaleSettingsForADevice.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Scale Settings for a Device","h":[["Steps-for-Setting-the-Scale","Steps for Setting the Scale"],["Step-1-Identify-a-Starting-Scale-Value","Step 1 - Identify a Starting Scale Value"],["Metric-and-Multi-start-Leadscrews","Metric and Multi-start Leadscrews"],["Step-2-Measure-the-Actual-Movement","Step 2 - Measure the Actual Movement"],["Step-3-Tune-the-Settings","Step 3 - Tune the Settings"],["An-Actual-Case","An Actual Case"],["Recommendation","Recommendation"]],"x":"When working to tune your axis’ settings, you will use the Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning tab of the Rosetta Settings program. Note : the Settings program cannot run while Rosetta (LinuxCNC) is running. So each round of tuning is: move and measure the axis in Rosetta, exit it, change the Scale in the Settings program, click Save All Settings on the Axis Selection tab, close the Settings program, then start Rosetta again and re-measure. Steps for Setting the Scale The axis' scale is used by LinuxCNC to know how to convert your distance measurement (i.e., inches, mm, or degrees) into pulses sent to a stepper motor for rotation. It is also used for speed (i.e., rpm) on the spindles. Step 1 - Identify a Starting Scale Value Use the table below to identify a starting scale value. Axis Unit of Measure Drive Type Comments X pulses / inch (any) Calculating the Gearing Ratio If the motor has a 20 tooth drive pulley on it, and the cross slide’s handwheel has an 120-tooth driven pulley, the gearing ratio is: Calculating the Leadscrew Ratio If each turn of the hand wheel on the cross slide moves the cutter 0.100” (i.e., 1 complete revolution of the handwheel moves the cutter 0.100”), the leadscrew ratio is: Calculating the Scale The settings on the StepperOnline DM542T Stepper Drive are set to: Using the gearing and the leadscrew ratios to identify the starting scale value gives: Z pulses / inch (any) Follow the directions outlined for the X axis. Do note that there will probably be differences between a cross slide and the linear drive on a curvilinear slide. You will need to ensure the values are correct for each (and they will probably be different). U, V, or W pulses / inch (any) Follow the directions outlined for the X axis. B pulses / degree Direct or Geared Multiply the noted value by the gearing ratio as outlined above for the X axis. This will of course need to be adjusted using the gearing ratio: Leadscrew Follow the directions outlined for the X axis for a leadscrew. If there is a gearing ratio for the driving of the leadscrew, follow those directions outlined for the X axis also. Sp0 Sp1 pulses / revolution (any) For the MDF Rose Engine stepper motor bracket designed by Jon Magill, the gearing ratio of the stepper motor’s drive pulley to the spindle’s driven pulley is 12 : 108 so the starting value will need to be multiplied by 108 ÷ 12 = 9 (9 is the value used in the ELFOS application) This value does not have to be exact for speed, but it needs to be very exact as it also sets the indexing operations for the spindle and the rosette phaser/multipler. Steps 2 & 3 below are really key for this. Metric and Multi-start Leadscrews A leadscrew's lead - how far one turn moves the slide - is its pitch × its number of starts. A 2mm pitch leadscrew with 4 starts moves 8mm a turn, so it needs a quarter of the pulses of a single-start one. Count the starts before working out the Scale, and test with a small move first (step 2 below). The Ornamental Turning Book of Knowledge's Leadscrew page explains pitch, lead and starts, and has tables of the pulses per mm and per inch for common metric and imperial leadscrews at 6,400 pulses per motor turn. Step 2 - Measure the Actual Movement The process for identifying the actual movement achieved is: Enable the axis you will be using. Setup a measuring device (i.e., a dial indicator ) to identify how far the axis actually moves. Using the Rosetta system, set the axis to move a small distance. If the motor is driving the axis in the wrong direction, reverse the polarity of the Scale (i.e., make a + into a -, or a - into a +). If you think the entered scale value is the right one to move one inch, test it by moving it 0.100” or even 0.010”. This is a good practice as the scale value you entered could be off by an order of magnitude, and you could easily end up crashing the device. This is especially the case where leadscrews have multiple starts. A leadscrew with a 2mm pitch and 4 starts would actually move 8mm for each revolution (i.e., 4 times the distance you may have thought at first). Once you are satisfied that the magnitude is right, set the movement for the axis to be a distance which makes sense for the device. A good starting point is 0.100” (especially as that is one complete revolution on a dial indicator ). Use the measured movement in the next step. Step 3 - Tune the Settings The values noted above should get very close to the expected movement for a selected axis. But, it is important to ensure the observed movement matches the calculated movement. To do this, Adjust the Scale value as necessary. This formulas below can help with determining the new values. An Actual Case The value from the table above notes that the cross slide value should be Axis = 384,000 pulses/inch For my cross slide, the calculated value was different than the actual value. Additionally, the two axes were not the same. The values were calibrated using a dial indicator to be: X Axis = 387,650 pulses/inch Z Axis = 387,100 pulses/inch Recommendation Once you determine the correct value for a given device, attach a label to that device with the value(s) needed. If you do not want to use a sticky label (e.g., from a Dymo label printer), then attach a string tag to the device. For example, my Retractor has this label attached to it. Rosetta O/S Scale -67,000.00 The \",\" will not be used when entering the value into the menu, but it makes it easier to ensure it is correctly entered. Rosetta O/S = Rosetta Operating System"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/SignalTower.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Signal Tower","h":[["What-It-Tells-You","What It Tells You"],["Why-It-Is-Useful","Why It Is Useful"],["What-to-Buy","What to Buy"]],"x":"Signal Tower A signal tower is a small column of coloured lights, usually also with a buzzer. It is useful to have when running longer operations on Rosetta as it shows at a glance the system’s current status (e.g., is it still running or not). And they LED lights are typically bright enough that they can be seen from across the shop. Rosetta is also configured to illuminate the red light if a typical error is encountered. The presence of a buzzer is useful as it can alert you if you are not looking in that direction. This is especially useful if an error is encountered. Use of a signal tower is optional: everything works without one. What It Tells You Signal What it means Red Light Something went wrong and your attention to the system is needed: an axis had a following error, a limit switch tripped, or the Base Box or Expansion Box lost contact with the Raspberry Pi (see Lost contact ). This lamp stays lit until you resolve the error and re- power on Rosetta. Green Light The machine is working on a task which could be lengthy. These are the operations setup to illuminate the green light: a spindle is turning, an indexing move, a synchronized movement , a threading movement , an MDI command is running, or a Gcode program is running. Other activities (e.g., jogging ) do not illuminate this light. Siren A siren which is used to alert you to certain conditions are achieved: A condition is triggered which illuminates the red light → 3-second siren sounds Tasks outlined which trigger the green light to illuminate are completed → 3 short beeps Gcode program encounters an M0 or M1 (Pause) command → 2 short beeps Both lights off The machine is idle, finished, or turned off. The full list of what does, and does not, turn each light on is in Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds - Signal Tower . The buzzer's sounds are explained in Sounds . Why It Is Useful Long programs. A rose engine pattern can run for hours. This enables you to work elsewhere in the shop and see from a distance whether the machine is still running (green), has finished (dark), or has stopped with a problem (red). Hear it, even when you can't see it. The buzzer tells you the moment something goes wrong, or when a job is done, without watching the screen. Problems stay visible. A following error, a tripped limit switch or a lost connection to a box can be over in an instant. The red light stays on so you know it happened, even if you were not there to see it. What to Buy The signal tower must be: Specification Requirement Voltage 24 volts DC (VDC) . The voltage supplied to this from Rosetta is 24 VDC. Lights At least a green and a red light. Other colours (such as yellow or blue) may be on the tower, but they will not be used. Buzzer Built in, with a steady (continuous) tone. A buzzer that pulses on its own will blur the short beeps together. Light Type LED, steady (not flashing). LED towers typically draw under 100 mA per light. Wiring The tower needs a separate wire for each light and the buzzer, plus one common wire. The cabling of the signal tower to Rosetta is outlined in signal tower cabling . Signal towers sold for machine tools (often listed as \"LED stack light\" or \"tower light 24V with buzzer\") meet these requirements."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/SignalTowerCabling.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Cabling a Signal Tower to Rosetta","h":[["Connector","Connector"],["Cable-Sizing","Cable Sizing"],["Wiring-the-Light","Wiring the Light"],["Signal-Tower-Notes","Signal Tower Notes"]],"x":"GX-12/4 Plug Connector A GX-12/4 female plug is used to connect the signal tower to the Rosetta system. This plug is sometimes called an aviation connector . Cable Sizing The cable between the Rosetta system and your signal tower should: Have 4 conductors within the cable Be stranded - this is more flexible than solid wire Be 22 AWG - this is easily soldered to the GX-12 connector. Check the tower's current rating; most LED towers draw under about 100 mA per lamp, which 22 AWG handles easily. Wiring the Light GX-12 Pin Wire Color Purpose 1 Green +V for green light 2 Red +V for red light 3 Blue +V for siren 4 Black Common GND Signal Tower Notes The signal tower plugs into the Base Box. The use of a signal tower must be configured on the Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab of the Rosetta Settings program. See Signal Tower for when each light and the buzzer come on."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/Sounds.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Sounds","h":[["The-Sounds-and-What-Makes-Them-Happen","The Sounds and What Makes Them Happen"],["Notes","Notes"],["Configuring-Sounds","Configuring Sounds"]],"x":"Rosetta is setup to make these sounds when certain conditions. The Sounds and What Makes Them Happen Sound Sounds when Does not sound when Can be Played On Signal Tower Monitor Speaker Both Fault siren One steady, 3-second sound An axis has a following error, A limit switch has tripped, or The Base Box or Expansion Box has lost contact. E-Stop is pressed, Machine is turned off, or Program is stopped. ✅ ✅ ✅ Done Three short beeps A Gcode program runs to its end. A Synchronized Movement (Run Operation) reaches its end point. A Cut Thread finishes all of its passes. The operation is stopped part way, by Stop, E-Stop, turning the machine off, or An error. Return to Start does not beep. ✅ ✅ ✅ Pause Two short beeps A Gcode program stops at an M0 or M1 and is waiting for you to continue it. You press the Pause button yourself. ✅ ✅ ✅ Startup abort sound Two short tones The gcode directory ① has one or more files using a reserved file name, and Startup File Check is set to Abort startup , or The gcode directory ① does not have files using a reserved file name. The gcode directory ① has one or more files using a reserved file name, and Startup File Check is set to Warn, but let me continue , or The gcode directory ① does not have files using a reserved file name. ❌ ② ✅ ❌ Notes The default gcode directory which is checked is: /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode/ This is due to these conditions for sounding an error are identified before LinuxCNC has started up and loaded the HAL; thusly, there is no ability to send a sound to the signal tower. The sound heard from the signal tower will typically be different from the sound heard on the monitor speaker. This is because there are options for changing the tone and/or harmonics on the monitor’s speaker, and these are not available on the signal tower (which is either on or off). Configuring Sounds The use of sounds with Rosetta is setup on the Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab in the Rosetta Settings program. The startup abort sound is different - it is set by the Startup File Check choice on the Other tab instead, since it can happen before Rosetta Settings' other sound choices are even running."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/Spindles.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Spindles","h":[["Traditional-Usage","Traditional Usage"],["Connections","Connections"],["Synchronized-Usage","Synchronized Usage"],["Connections_","Connections"]],"x":"Gcode Example m3 s5 $1 run the rosette phaser/multiplier ($1) in the forward direction (m3) at a feed rate of 5 rpm (s5) There are two spindles catered for on this system: Sp0 = the main spindle (also known as $0 with Gcode commands), and Sp1 = the rosette phaser/multiplier (also known as $1 with Gcode commands) However, there are two modes of operation for the spindles, and this is a system-driven function. Traditional Usage Stepper Motor Connections Using the configuration shown to the right, you can make a lot of things happen. For example, Using only Sp0 and Sp1, you can do the traditional stuff we do on rose engines (e.g., rocking & pumping). For rocking & pumping, X & Z would not be synchronized for movement. Using Sp0 and Z, you can make a Turks Head Move the cutter in (moving the Z axis by hand) Move the cutter out (moving the Z axis by hand) Index the Spindle (Sp0; using Rosetta’s index function) Repeat as necessary X & Sp1 would be unused. When the spindle is to be used as in a typical lathe operation, it may be used for two types of operations: Run Operation This is used to run the spindles as is done on most rose engines (e.g., for rocking or pumping). On Rosetta, the menu to the right is used (as shown in the picture to the right). These fields are used to determine how it operates. ENA is used to enable or disable the spindle's operation. If a command is sent to the spindle’s stepper motor, but it is not enabled, no operation will happen, and an error message will eventually popup (usually a “following error”). Note: If the setting Axis Not Enabled is set to Enable it automatically , then commands to run a motor will automatically ENAble the axis, and leave it ENAbled after the operation finishes. The speed for the spindle is called the feed rate . For Sp0 (main spindle), the maximum is 10 rpm, but that is probably too fast for most rose engine work. For Sp1 (rosette phaser/multiplier), the feed rate is set as a percentage of the Sp0 spindle. It has range of up to 1,000% (10x) of the speed of the Sp0 spindle. If it is set at a negative number, it runs in the opposite direction. If it is set to 0, it will not run. There are 3 buttons which are used next: Run in the forward direction, Run in the reverse direction, or Stop running. Indexing This is used to index the spindle(s) orientation as is done using an indexing wheel or a click plate. On Rosetta, the spindles to be indexed must be ENAbled. Note: If the setting Axis Not Enabled is set to Enable it automatically , then commands to run a motor will automatically ENAble the axis, and leave it ENAbled after the operation finishes. Then, the menu on the right side of the screen is used, and these fields are used to determine how it operates: Distance is a numeric value, and then the operator determines if this is: degrees, or divisions of a circle Which spindles are to be indexed. Select Sp0 to index the Sp0 index, Sp1 to index the Sp1 index, or Both Sp0 and Sp1 to index both spindles together. If none are selected, nothing will happen. There are 2 buttons which are used next: Index in the forward direction, or Index in the reverse direction. The stop button noted above will stop this if needed. Connections For this, the spindles are connected to whichever jacks are assigned to Sp0 (the main spindle) and Sp1 (the rosette phaser/multiplier) on the Axis Selection tab of the Settings program. By default, these are channels 04 and 05, the right-hand pair of plugs on the Base Box. Synchronized Usage Stepper Motor Connections If the spindle(s) need to have their movement synchronized with another axis (or axes), the spindle motors are not connected as above. Instead, each is run as a rotary axis (either A, B, or C). Using the configuration shown to the right, you can make threads (for example). X would typically be unused, though the threading function uses it to move the cutter away from / into the piece. This synchronized movement is performed via gCode, or from the main screen's Sync Move and Threading tabs. For threads, use the Threading tab. (LinuxCNC's own threading commands, G33 and G76, cannot be used on Rosetta - they expect the work to be turned by the Sp0 spindle, not by an axis such as C.) Connections For this, the spindles are connected to the jacks on the panel which are configured for use as rotary axes."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/StepperMotorCabling.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Cabling Stepper Motors to Rosetta","h":[["Cable-Connector","Cable Connector"],["Cable-Sizing","Cable Sizing"],["Wiring-the-Motors","Wiring the Motors"]],"x":"GX-16/4 Plug Cable Connector The connector used to attach a stepper motor to the Rosetta system is a GX-16/4 female plug. This plug is sometimes called an aviation connector . Cable Sizing The cable between the Rosetta system and your stepper motor should: Have 4 conductors within the cable Be stranded - this is more flexible than solid wire Be 20 AWG (gauge) - This will ensure the wire can easily handle the amperage for the NEMA 23 stepper motors, and is easily soldered to the GX-12 connector Be shielded, with the shield connected to ground at the Rosetta box end only - this will help isolate the signal from noise which could be encountered in adjacent cables (e.g., for stepper motors) Wiring the Motors GX-16 Pin Stepper Motor Comments Stepper Online Wire Color Purpose 1 Black A+ If the motor is driving the operation in the wrong direction, the recommended solution is to reverse the polarity of the Scale (i.e., make a positive number into a negative number, or vice versa). You could also swap the A+ and A- wires, but that is not recommended. 2 Green A- 3 Red B+ 4 Blue B-"},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/SwitchSettingsOnTheDM542Ts.html","m":"User Manual","t":"DIP Switch Settings on the DM542T Stepper Motor Drives","h":[["Stepper-Motor-Current-Amps","Stepper Motor Current (Amps)"],["Idle-Current","Idle Current"],["Microsteps-per-Revolution","Microsteps per Revolution"]],"x":"Rosetta uses DM542T Stepper Motor Drivers to make the stepper motors work. There is one for each channel inside the Rosetta case. These DM542Ts can have different configurations (DIP Switch Settings) set on them based on your needs. Notes Before opening the cover on the Rosetta box, ensure it is powered off and the power cable removed. This will help ensure you are not shocked. Do not change the 5V/24V switch on the side of the DM542T. If it is set to 24V, the channel will not work correctly to make your stepper motor work. Do not touch any components inside the box which have exposed places. Static electricity in your body can damage the electrical components. Stepper Motor Current (Amps) Depending on the stepper motor you are driving, you also may want to adjust the current being delivered to the motor. Some testing has shown that setting it too high can cause your stepper motor to heat up quite a bit. It probably won't overheat, but it can get quite hot. In particular, NEMA 17 stepper motors generally do not need as much current as NEMA 23 stepper motors. You can set the value closer to what is needed if you wish. Do note that setting it too low can cause the stepper motor to not work as expected. So test the values selected before committing a new setting for work on a prized piece. To change the current setting, you can change the values for the selected channel on the DM542 Stepper Motor Drive. Those switches are SW1 - SW3, and the value options are listed on page 6 in the User Manual for the DM542T . Changes to this will probably require changes to the scale settings for a device on the Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning tab of the Settings program. Idle Current We recommend you leave this at 50%. (That is SW4 set to off). Microsteps per Revolution When you run the program, Microstep Calculator , to identify the micro steps per revolution you want to use, you can change the values for the selected channel on the DM542 Stepper Motor Drive. As noted there, Higher microsteps per revolution : this gives more granularity of movement, though something below 0.0005” is probably not noticeable. Lower microsteps per revolution : this allows the stepper motor to run faster. This may be desired if you want to quickly move the position of the cutter or the object before engaging the cutter into the workpiece. Those switches are SW5 - SW8, and the value options are listed on page 6 in the User Manual for the DM542T ."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/Troubleshooting.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Troubleshooting","h":[["Starting-Up","Starting Up"],["Rosetta-won-t-start","Rosetta won't start"],["Base-box-lost-contact-E-stop-or-Expansion-box-lost-contact-E-stop","\"Base box lost contact - E-stop\" or \"Expansion box lost contact - E-stop\""],["General-Usage-of-the-System","General Usage of the System"],["An-axis-row-is-grayed-out-on-the-main-screen","An axis row is grayed out on the main screen"],["The-E-stop-won-t-clear-F1-does-nothing","The E-stop won't clear (F1 does nothing)"],["Unable-to-jog-the-rosette-phaser-multiplier-spindle-Sp1","Unable to jog the rosette phaser/multiplier spindle (Sp1)"],["I-changed-a-setting-but-nothing-changed-in-Rosetta","I changed a setting, but nothing changed in Rosetta"],["A-G-code-program-won-t-load-or-run","A G-code program won't load or run"],["The-Threading-tab-won-t-run","The Threading tab won't run"],["Error-Messages","Error Messages"],["Joint-following-error-joint-0-following-error","Joint following error (\"joint 0 following error\")"],["Latency-error-Unexpected-realtime-delay-or-error-finishing-read","Latency error (\"Unexpected realtime delay...\" or \"error finishing read\")"],["The-Pi-is-warm-or-too-hot-Raspberry-Pi-is-warm-or-Raspberry-Pi-is-too-hot","The Pi is warm or too hot (\"Raspberry Pi is warm\" or \"Raspberry Pi is too hot\")"],["The-power-supply-isn-t-keeping-up-undervoltage","\"The power supply isn't keeping up (undervoltage)\""],["Limit-Switches-and-Signal-Tower","Limit Switches and Signal Tower"],["An-axis-stopped-on-a-limit-switch","An axis stopped on a limit switch"],["The-signal-tower-s-red-light-is-on-or-the-buzzer-sounded","The signal tower's red light is on, or the buzzer sounded"],["The-signal-tower-lights-or-buzzer-are-on-when-Rosetta-isn-t-running","The signal tower lights or buzzer are on when Rosetta isn't running"],["Settings-Program","Settings Program"],["The-Settings-program-won-t-open","The Settings program won't open"],["Tuning-changes-were-lost","Tuning changes were lost"],["A-limit-switch-s-jack-shows-not-assigned-cleared","A limit switch's jack shows \"not assigned - cleared\""],["A-jog-speed-change-was-refused-and-put-back","A jog speed change was refused and put back"],["Sp1-can-t-be-assigned-to-a-channel","Sp1 can't be assigned to a channel"],["Phone-alerts-don-t-arrive","Phone alerts don't arrive"]],"x":"Find your problem in the list below, or look for the exact words of the error message on your screen. Starting Up: Rosetta won't start - \"lost contact - E-stop\" General Usage: Axis row grayed out - E-stop won't clear - Can't jog Sp1 - A setting change had no effect - G-code program won't run - Threading tab won't run Error Messages: Joint following error - Latency error Limit Switches and Signal Tower: Stopped on a limit switch - Red light / buzzer - Tower on when Rosetta is off Settings Program: Settings won't open - Tuning changes lost - \"not assigned - cleared\" - Jog speed change refused - Can't assign Sp1 - Phone alerts don't arrive Starting Up Rosetta won't start If Expansion Box - Connected is ticked on the Axis Selection tab, Rosetta looks for the Expansion Box when it starts, and won't start if it can't find it. Make sure both boxes are switched on before starting Rosetta, and that the Expansion Box's network cable is plugged into the Base Box. If you don't have an Expansion Box, or aren't using it today, untick Connected , then start Rosetta again. The same applies to the Base Box: it must be switched on before Rosetta is started. \"Base box lost contact - E-stop\" or \"Expansion box lost contact - E-stop\" The Raspberry Pi stopped hearing from that box while Rosetta was running, so it put the system into E-stop. Check that box's network cable, the network switch, and the box's power, then press F1. See Lost contact with a box . General Usage of the System An axis row is grayed out on the main screen That axis isn't assigned to a channel (plug), or it is assigned to an Expansion Box channel (06 - 09) while the Expansion Box isn't ticked as Connected . Assign it on the Axis Selection tab of the Settings program, then restart Rosetta. The E-stop won't clear (F1 does nothing) If an E-stop button is set as connected in the Settings program: the E-stop can't be cleared while the button is pressed, and it can't be cleared when nothing is plugged into the E-stop plug. Release the button (or plug an E-stop button in), then press F1. If you don't have an E-stop button, make sure E-Stop Button - Connected is unchecked on the Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab of the Settings program. If a box lost contact, F1 works again once that box is back in contact - see lost contact . Unable to jog the rosette phaser/multiplier spindle (Sp1) The F3 Manual Control / Jogging screen can't be used to jog that spindle. It is the only axis which can't be jogged, because of the way LinuxCNC's AXIS screen is built. I changed a setting, but nothing changed in Rosetta Changes made in the Settings program take effect the next time Rosetta starts . Because the Settings program can't run at the same time as Rosetta, this normally happens on its own. If you don't see the change: make sure the change was saved (see Tuning changes were lost ), then close Rosetta completely and start it again. A G-code program won't load or run The most common reason is that the program moves an axis that isn't assigned to a channel (plug). For example, a program that moves C won't run if C isn't assigned. Look at which axis letters the program uses (the G-code library lists them for each program). Assign those axes on the Axis Selection tab of the Settings program, then restart Rosetta. The Threading tab won't run Threading turns the work with the C axis, not with spindle Sp0, so C must be assigned to a channel. For a threading setup, put C on the plug the main spindle drive is connected to (by default, Sp0 is on channel 04), in place of Sp0. Assign it on the Axis Selection tab of the Settings program, then restart Rosetta. Error Messages Generally, these messages are seen on the lower right-hand corner of the screen when using LinuxCNC. Joint following error (\"joint 0 following error\") What it means LinuxCNC is always checking that each axis is where it was told to be. If an axis falls too far behind, LinuxCNC turns the machine off and reports a following error. The number in the message is the joint, not the plug. What it does not mean It rarely means the motor or drive is damaged. It doesn't mean the G-code program is wrong. Common causes The axis is stalled or jammed, or something is in its way. The feed rate is too fast for the device. Max Vel or Max Accel is set too high for the motor and the load it is moving. A loose motor cable. What to do Clear the error (click on the X). Check that the axis moves freely by hand, and that nothing is in its way. Power on (F2) and try again. If it keeps happening, reduce the feed rate, or lower the Max Vel and Max Accel values on Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning . Another guide which may be of use is LinuxCNC Stepper Diagnostics . Latency error (\"Unexpected realtime delay...\" or \"error finishing read\") What it means LinuxCNC has to send a steady stream of small instructions to the motor controller board, on a strict schedule, many times every second. If something on the computer delays it, or the instructions are slow getting through the network cables to the boards, the motors can't be kept in step. LinuxCNC then stops the machine and reports a latency error, rather than risk a distorted pattern. On a rose engine, the motions have to stay in step with each other (for example, the spindle rotation and the rubber or cutter movement). If the instructions arrive late, the motions drift apart and the pattern gets distorted. Stopping protects your workpiece and your cutter. What it does not mean It usually doesn't mean the machine is broken. It doesn't mean your pattern or settings are wrong. It is almost always the computer being briefly busy, or a connection problem - not a mechanical one. Common causes Other programs on the computer using a lot of processing power. One of particular note is Firefox, especially with busy web sites open (e.g., Google mail). A loose or damaged network cable between the Raspberry Pi, the network switch in the Base Box, and the controller boards. Something else plugged into that network switch (a router, or another computer). Only the Raspberry Pi and the boxes may be connected to it. WiFi activity on the Raspberry Pi. What to do Close Firefox and any other programs, clear the error, then power on (F2). Check that the network cables are firmly plugged in at both ends, and that nothing other than the Raspberry Pi and the boxes is plugged into the network switch. If the error keeps happening, note when it occurs (at startup, at a certain point in a pattern, or at random) and email us at ColvinTools@Gmail.com . The Pi is warm or too hot (\"Raspberry Pi is warm\" or \"Raspberry Pi is too hot\") What it means The temperature at the top of the main screen has reached the Warning (orange) or Alarm (red) temperature set in Rosetta Settings . Near 85 °C the Pi slows itself down, and that can cause a following error or a latency error part way through a program. Rosetta does not stop anything - it is a warning. What to do Check that the box's vents aren't blocked and nothing is stacked on or against it. Check that the Pi's fan is turning. Keep the box out of direct sun and away from heaters; a cooler room helps. Close Firefox and any other programs you aren't using - they make the Pi work harder. If it keeps happening, email us at ColvinTools@Gmail.com . The highest temperature is sent to us each time you update the software, which helps us see what happened. \"The power supply isn't keeping up (undervoltage)\" What it means The Raspberry Pi's 5V supply dropped too low for a moment, and a ⚡ is shown beside the temperature on the main screen. It is often brief - for example, when the motors all start at once - but a Pi short of power can behave like a hot one. What to do If it happens only now and then, nothing needs to be done. If it keeps happening, check the 5V power supply and its wiring and plugs, and email us at ColvinTools@Gmail.com . Limit Switches and Signal Tower An axis stopped on a limit switch When a limit switch is tripped, LinuxCNC turns the machine off and shows an error. Clear the error (click on the X), then power on (F2). If the switch is set as a min or max limit, jog the axis away from the switch. If the switch is set as both ends , check Override Limits in LinuxCNC's Machine menu, carefully jog the axis off the switch (away from it), then uncheck Override Limits. If a limit trips when no switch is touched, check the cable and connector - a broken or loose wire reads the same as a tripped switch. Also make sure any Limit Jack with nothing plugged in is set to Not used , because an empty normally closed (NC) jack reads the same as a tripped switch. If a limit jack shows \"not assigned - cleared\" in the Settings program, see that entry below. The signal tower's red light is on, or the buzzer sounded The red light means LinuxCNC stopped the machine because of a joint following error , a tripped limit switch , or a box that lost contact . The buzzer sounds for a few seconds when the red light comes on. See those entries for what to do. The red light goes off when the machine is powered on again (F2). Pressing the E-stop or stopping a program does not turn on the red light. The signal tower lights or buzzer are on when Rosetta isn't running The tower's relay board must be the type that switches on when its input is pulled low (\"active low\"). The controller board holds its outputs high whenever Rosetta isn't running (while starting up and after it exits), so a board that switches on when its input is high turns everything on at those times. Use an active-low relay board. (The Rosetta system we ship already comes with this type of relays.) Settings Program The Settings program won't open The Settings program can't run while Rosetta is running. Close Rosetta first (click on the X in the top right of its screen, and wait a minute for it to finish shutting down), then open the Settings program. Tuning changes were lost Scale, Max Vel, Max Accel, and PID changes are only saved when you click Save All Settings on the Axis Selection tab, or choose Save and Close when the Settings program asks as you close it. If Close Without Saving was chosen, those changes are discarded. See Saving and Sharing Settings . All other settings (channel assignments, Expansion Box Connected, limit jacks, E-stop, signal tower, backlash, jog speeds, measurement system and device names) are saved as soon as you change them. A limit switch's jack shows \"not assigned - cleared\" The axis that jack was set to is no longer assigned to a channel (plug), so the jack was set back to Not used . Assign the axis to a channel, then choose it for the jack again. A jog speed change was refused and put back In each group of jog speeds, Min must be no more than Default , and Default must be no more than Max . A change that breaks this is refused and the old value is put back. Change the values in an order that keeps them in that order - for example, to raise all three, change Max first, then Default, then Min. Sp1 can't be assigned to a channel Sp1 (the rosette phaser/multiplier spindle) always runs as a percentage of Sp0's speed, so Sp1 can only be assigned when Sp0 is also assigned to a channel. Assign Sp0 first, then Sp1. Phone alerts don't arrive In Rosetta Settings, on the Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab, press Send Test Message , and look at what it says under the button: Not sent: couldn't reach the ntfy server - the Raspberry Pi isn't on the internet. Check its Wi-Fi (see Setting up Wi-Fi ). Sent - check your phone , but nothing arrives - the topic in the ntfy app doesn't match the one in Rosetta Settings. Check every letter: capital letters matter. Also check that your phone allows notifications from the ntfy app. If the test message arrives but alerts from the machine don't: Phone alerts take effect the next time Rosetta starts. Close Rosetta and start it again after changing them. Check that the event is ticked under Send When . Finished is only sent when a program, Sync Move or Cut Thread finishes - not when you stop it part way."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/UpdateRosetta.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Updating the Rosetta System","h":[["Updating-Rosetta","Updating Rosetta"],["Using-the-Applications-menu","Using the Applications menu"],["Additional-Notes","Additional Notes"]],"x":"To update the software on your Rosetta system, you will need to be connected to the Internet. This is typically done via your home Wi-Fi network ( Wi-Fi setup instructions ). Do NOT connect anything to your Rosetta system using the network switch inside the box. This could cause performance issues on the system. Once you are connected to the Internet, you can update your Rosetta system. Updating Rosetta To update the Rosetta program, use Update Rosetta on the Applications menu. The update program will download any available upgrades for the following: Updates to the Rosetta code Any changes and updates to the config files within Rosetta Updates to LinuxCNC Updates to the operating system ( Debian Linux ) Updates to the virus scanning system ( ClamAV ) and then apply these upgrades to your system. Using the Applications menu Click on Applications at the top left corner of the screen Click on the arrow to the right of Rosetta Click on Update Rosetta (the one with a green up arrow) Applications Rosetta > Rosetta Rosetta Settings Backup Rosetta Update Rosetta Geometric Chuck Designer Rosette Designer Note: It is a good practice to restart your computer after updating it. Close Rosetta, use Shut Down at the bottom of the Applications menu, wait until the screen has gone dark and then 10 seconds more, then switch the power off and back on. Additional Notes If you run the update program too soon after starting up your system, you may encounter an error. This is because the virus scanning is still running. Wait 30 minutes and run it again. This should clear up any issues."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/WiFi.html","m":"User Manual","t":"Setting up Wi-Fi","h":[],"x":"WiFi Icon To setup Wi-Fi on your Rosetta system, click on the Wi-Fi icon located on the title bar at the top of the system. It is the one indicated on the image to the right. From there, you setup Wi-Fi just as you do on other computers."},{"u":"../Rosetta_User_Manual/index.html","m":"User Manual","t":"User Manual","h":[],"x":"Overview of Rosetta Getting started with Rosetta How the Rosetta programs fit together Starting up your new system Switching the system off Setting up Wi-Fi Cabling E-Stop Button Cabling Limit Switch & Pause Button Cabling Signal Tower Cabling Stepper Motor Cabling Axes used in Rosetta Some key notes about using Rosetta Entering Data Spindles Updating the Rosetta software Configuring Rosetta’s Settings Axis Selection Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds E-Stop Button Limit Switches Pause Button Phone Alerts Signal Tower Sounds Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning Scale Settings for a Device Microstep Calculator Other Configuration Settings Saving and Sharing Settings DIP Switch Settings on the DM542T Stepper Motor Drives Using Rosetta Main Screen Indexing Synchronized Movement Threading Using ENAble to control motor movements Running Gcode Programs Function Keys for LinuxCNC F1 - E-Stop F2 - Power on F3 - Manual Control / Jogging F5 - MDI Tab Ctrl-Home - Home All Axes Signal Tower Sounds Troubleshooting ℛ Help Designing Patterns Rosette Designer - software-defined rosettes Geometric Chuck Designer - software-defined geometric chucks Backing Up Key User Files Backing Up Key User Files to a USB Flash Drive Restoring Key User Files from a USB Flash Drive Updating Rosetta Updating the Rosetta software Rosetta Software Release Notes Other Useful Information Ornamental Turners Handy Book Using an AI with Rosetta 3D Printable Parts Rosetta Gcode Library User Forum LinuxCNC Gcode Quick Reference LinuxCNC Manual Other PID Controller (Wikipedia) The Support Manual is where we compile technical and support documentation about the LinuxCNC system, the Rosetta configuration, and other topics. It is not recommended for the general user, but may be of assistance for technical questions or complex problems."},{"u":"BasketWeave.html","m":"Gcode Library","t":"Basket Weave","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["Program-Designer","Program Designer"],["Gcode","Gcode"],["How-it-Works","How it Works"],["Approach-Options","Approach Options"],["Axes-Movement-Notes","Axes & Movement Notes"],["User-Data-to-be-Entered","User Data to be Entered"]],"x":"This still untested Basket Weave Pattern Purpose This program is to create a basket weave pattern as shown in the picture to the right Program Designer If you have any questions, contact Rich Colvin Gcode Download BasketWeave.ngc Save it in Rosetta’s Gcode folder, ~/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode . If the browser asks where to save it, click Gcode in the list on the left; if it saves it straight to your Downloads folder, move it from there into the Gcode folder. How it Works The activity is: Loop 1 - Repeat for the number of rows of patterns Loop 2 - Repeat for the number of cuts per wave Plunge the cutter in to the piece Cut a zig-zag pattern around the piece. Loop 3 - Repeat for the number of waves around the piece Cut a zig Cut a zag Withdraw the cutter from the piece For each cut for this wave, Reposition the cutter for the next cut in the wave Once all the cuts for this wave are complete, reposition the cutter for the next wave Reverse the cutting direction for the cuts per wave. Approach Options The approach options for using this code are: Select the end of the piece to start making the cuts: Start from the headstock end, and move towards the tailstock end. (DEFAULT) Start from the tailstock end, and move towards the headstock end. Determine what equipment to use for handling the cutter plunge/withdraw Use the X axis on the cross slide. This actually plunges the cutter into / out from the piece. (DEFAULT) Use the retractor (using the U axis). This approach keeps the cutter fixed, and moves the headstock away from, or in to the cutter. Axes & Movement Notes Axis Usage Comments X or U The X or U axis can be used. Plunge the cutter into the piece for cutting, and Withdraw the cutter away from the cylinder when repositioning it. When using the X axis , the cutter is plunged into / withdrawn from the piece. When using the U axis , the headstock is released to allow the piece to plunge into the cutter, or pushed away to withdraw it from the cutter. Ensure the backlash setting for this axis is set appropriately. Z This axis is used for movement along the lathe bed. Ensure the backlash setting for this axis is set appropriately. C This axis is used for driving the spindle rotation. Ensure the backlash setting for this axis is set appropriately. Ensure the scale setting for this axis is set appropriately. Typically, the scale is set to match the value for Sp0. User Data to be Entered The user will need to set these data: Variable Data to set Pattern Options #1 The diameter of the piece. #2 Number of waves (the number of boxes around the perimeter). #3 The number of cuts per wave (in the picture to the right, there are 3). #4 The number of rows of Wave patterns. Every other row is reversed. Cutter Plunge Options #10 Cutter plunge option 1 = Use X axis for cutter plunge (DEFAULT) 2 = Use U axis (headstock) for cutter plunge X Axis Options #20 Feed rate for X or U axis cutter withdrawal #21 Feed rate for X or U axis cutter plunge #22 Distance for X or U axis cutter plunge C Axis (Spindle) Options #30 Spindle feed rate during repositioning Z Axis Options #40 Z axis feed rate during repositioning C & Z (Synchronized) Axes Options #50 C & Z axes feed rate during synchronized cutting #51 C & Z axes feed rate during repositioning Other Options #60 Starting side 1 = left; headstock side, moving towards tailstock (DEFAULT) 2 = right; tailstock side, moving towards headstock"},{"u":"Curvilinear.html","m":"Gcode Library","t":"Curvilinear","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["Program-Designer","Program Designer"],["Gcode","Gcode"],["How-it-Works","How it Works"],["Axes-Movement-Notes","Axes & Movement Notes"],["User-Data-to-be-Entered","User Data to be Entered"]],"x":"This still untested Purpose This program was developed to support experiments with a curvilinear slide. It is quite general. It moves the cutter over, retracts it, returns and then does it again. It is structured with variables to be easily reconfigured. Program Designer If you have any questions, contact Chuck Peplinski Gcode Download n_pass.ngc Save it in Rosetta’s Gcode folder, ~/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/gcode . If the browser asks where to save it, click Gcode in the list on the left; if it saves it straight to your Downloads folder, move it from there into the Gcode folder. How it Works I developed this using Claude AI to control my experiments with a curvilinear pattern. Variables at the head of the file make it easy to reconfigure. Starting from home, the cutter stays in place (dwells) for a full rotation, then we move along the Z axis to the end (z_stroke) where we stay in place (dwell) for a full rotation. Upon reaching the end we retract on the X axis. If n_pass is more than one we return to the start, reengage the X axis more deeply, and do it again. Note that z_stroke can be negative to move right to left. For curvilinear work it is advantageous to use a higher sp0_rpm (maybe 3 or 4) and a very slow movement on the Z axis. Approach Options Axes & Movement Notes Axis Usage Comments X or U (what is this axis used for?) (comments) User Data to be Entered The user will need to set these data: Variable Data to set #1 (what this is used for)"},{"u":"GCodeReferences.html","m":"Gcode Library","t":"GCode References","h":[["Good-Online-Guides","Good Online Guides"],["Good-Physical-Books","Good Physical Books"]],"x":"Good Online Guides LinuxCNC Manuals (HTML) Gcode Overview Gcode Examples G Codes M Codes O Codes RS274/NGC Differences Good Physical Books Machinery’s Handbook , by Erik Oberg. Later editions (e.g., 31+) have good reference information."},{"u":"Threading-SingleStart.html","m":"Gcode Library","t":"Threading - Single Start","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["Code","Code"],["Code-Comments","Code Comments"]],"x":"Purpose This gCode is used to establish threads on a piece. Code ; Make single-start threads ; The value of Z is the distance to cut the threads (in this case, 0.25 inches) ; The value of K should be 1/TPI (in this case, 16 TPI = 0.0625) G33 Z0.25 K0.0625 ; ; If the cross slide is connected to X also, these codes can reposition the cutter back to the beginning position. ; Move the cutter along the X axis out from the threads (towards the user) by 0.5 inches G0 X0.5 ; Move the cutter along the Z axis in the opposite direction of the threading by 0.25 inches G0 Z-0.25 ; Move the cutter along the X axis to put it back into place G0 X-0.5 Code Comments G33 is only used for single-start threads. For multi-start threads, G76 is needed."},{"u":"index.html","m":"Gcode Library","t":"Index","h":[],"x":"gCode References B Basket Weave C Curvilinear T Threading - Single Start (G33) The Rosetta User Manual and the Rosetta Support Manual may be of use also."}];
